1
00:01:39,436 --> 00:01:41,404
- Fill us in.
- Was it shocking?

2
00:01:41,405 --> 00:01:43,105
- Was it shocking?
- I'm dying to know.

3
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:46,675
- Could you brief us, Reporter?
- Brief you?

4
00:01:49,713 --> 00:01:52,448
Why are you putting me through this?

5
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:54,750
It's okay. No pressure.

6
00:01:55,810 --> 00:01:57,319
I did what I could.

7
00:02:03,293 --> 00:02:05,027
I'm putting an embargo on this.

8
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:07,530
An embargo?

9
00:02:09,999 --> 00:02:13,535
(Embargo: a temporary ban
on releasing information)

10
00:02:15,145 --> 00:02:16,205
Right off the bat?

11
00:02:18,976 --> 00:02:21,243
Are you tearing up?

12
00:02:21,778 --> 00:02:23,545
- Why are you about to cry?
- What's with the tears?

13
00:02:24,115 --> 00:02:25,214
It's nothing.

14
00:02:26,316 --> 00:02:27,316
It's just so heartbreaking.

15
00:02:27,317 --> 00:02:28,484
Seriously.

16
00:02:28,485 --> 00:02:31,320
I'm so confused. Why are you crying?

17
00:02:31,555 --> 00:02:33,223
Well, it's just...

18
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:35,158
I know, right? Don't worry about it.

19
00:02:45,368 --> 00:02:46,601
An embargo...

20
00:02:46,602 --> 00:02:49,705
So you're embargoing
the facts you confirmed.

21
00:02:49,706 --> 00:02:50,873
Can I ask something?

22
00:02:50,874 --> 00:02:53,442
We put together that list of questions.

23
00:02:53,443 --> 00:02:55,711
- Did you get answers to all of them?
- Did you get them all answered?

24
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:58,915
(Ten minutes ago,
finalizing the questions)

25
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:00,849
(Who is the spy?)

26
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,685
That information cannot be disclosed.

27
00:03:03,386 --> 00:03:08,023
(Questions to identify the spy)

28
00:03:08,404 --> 00:03:10,226
- I did.
- Really?

29
00:03:10,227 --> 00:03:12,508
I asked the questions we prepared.

30
00:03:12,509 --> 00:03:13,964
(Didn't get answers
to the prepared questions)

31
00:03:13,965 --> 00:03:16,265
How long is the embargo for?

32
00:03:16,266 --> 00:03:17,767
When does it lift?

33
00:03:17,768 --> 00:03:20,001
Usually, embargoes have a set deadline.

34
00:03:22,607 --> 00:03:26,075
Until the issue isn't sensitive anymore.

35
00:03:26,076 --> 00:03:27,677
Will this ever not be sensitive?

36
00:03:27,678 --> 00:03:30,079
So could it last more than a day?

37
00:03:30,614 --> 00:03:33,282
- The embargo.
- It's not just a matter of a day or two.

38
00:03:33,783 --> 00:03:35,717
- It could take longer than that.
- Well...

39
00:03:35,952 --> 00:03:36,919
In that case...

40
00:03:40,590 --> 00:03:41,523
But why?

41
00:03:42,092 --> 00:03:44,393
Shouldn't she at least tell us why?

42
00:03:44,394 --> 00:03:45,428
It was so frustrating.

43
00:03:45,962 --> 00:03:47,229
Why won't she tell us?

44
00:03:47,297 --> 00:03:49,131
Is this why she wanted to be the Reporter?

45
00:03:51,735 --> 00:03:54,203
We just want...

46
00:03:54,204 --> 00:03:56,872
to have some idea of what's going on.

47
00:03:57,474 --> 00:03:58,374
That's all.

48
00:03:58,742 --> 00:04:00,643
We're completely in the dark.

49
00:04:00,644 --> 00:04:02,378
You went in there...

50
00:04:02,379 --> 00:04:04,046
to bring back intel.

51
00:04:04,246 --> 00:04:08,283
We haven't actually accomplished anything.

52
00:04:14,091 --> 00:04:16,692
She was probably just turning
on the waterworks.

53
00:04:17,026 --> 00:04:19,395
If you take the emotion out of it
and look at it objectively,

54
00:04:19,462 --> 00:04:22,764
she's the prime suspect...

55
00:04:22,947 --> 00:04:24,466
for being the spy.

56
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:26,235
The Reporter was supposed to give us...

57
00:04:26,236 --> 00:04:29,405
a fighting chance against the spy.

58
00:04:29,406 --> 00:04:31,306
Instead, all she's doing is...

59
00:04:31,307 --> 00:04:33,642
leaving us completely powerless.

60
00:04:34,244 --> 00:04:36,478
She isn't a Reporter working...

61
00:04:36,479 --> 00:04:39,148
for some news outlet
and making money that way.

62
00:04:39,149 --> 00:04:41,150
We're the ones who elected her.

63
00:04:41,151 --> 00:04:42,685
We gave her that authority,

64
00:04:42,686 --> 00:04:45,988
so she should answer to us.

65
00:04:45,989 --> 00:04:48,825
An embargo just means
holding the story for a bit.

66
00:04:48,826 --> 00:04:51,594
She'll have to tell us eventually.

67
00:04:51,595 --> 00:04:54,630
But as an elected journalist,
she has a duty to fulfill.

68
00:04:54,631 --> 00:04:55,965
(Right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

69
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:58,800
(Day Three activities have concluded)

70
00:04:58,801 --> 00:05:00,869
How much can you share?

71
00:05:00,870 --> 00:05:02,804
We won't pry into the actual questions.

72
00:05:02,805 --> 00:05:04,639
But I do have one question.

73
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,309
We should at least know...

74
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:08,677
- Exactly.
- why she put an embargo on it.

75
00:05:08,678 --> 00:05:10,745
I get that she can't share...

76
00:05:11,081 --> 00:05:12,547
the information right now.

77
00:05:12,548 --> 00:05:13,916
Is this to protect us...

78
00:05:13,917 --> 00:05:15,685
- or yourself?
- What is the embargo even for?

79
00:05:15,686 --> 00:05:18,287
You owe us an explanation.

80
00:05:18,288 --> 00:05:20,723
Why you embargoed it
and when you'll lift it.

81
00:05:20,858 --> 00:05:22,458
We deserve to know that much.

82
00:05:22,759 --> 00:05:26,395
Can't you even tell us your reasoning...

83
00:05:26,396 --> 00:05:27,930
behind it?

84
00:05:31,067 --> 00:05:33,236
Do you think this would've happened...

85
00:05:33,237 --> 00:05:35,137
if someone else were the Reporter?

86
00:05:36,239 --> 00:05:38,273
That's hard to say.
There are too many variables.

87
00:05:38,441 --> 00:05:41,576
Even if they went in
with the exact same questions.

88
00:05:42,145 --> 00:05:44,113
That would be up to them.

89
00:05:44,114 --> 00:05:45,214
It'd be their call.

90
00:05:54,624 --> 00:05:57,159
The Reporter's silence throws...

91
00:05:57,761 --> 00:05:59,962
the community into disarray.

92
00:06:05,101 --> 00:06:07,001
Tomorrow is going to be brutal.

93
00:06:07,002 --> 00:06:09,905
This took a turn I never saw coming.

94
00:06:09,906 --> 00:06:10,906
Were we...

95
00:06:12,208 --> 00:06:13,508
too fixated...

96
00:06:15,145 --> 00:06:16,212
on everyone surviving together?

97
00:06:22,352 --> 00:06:24,386
What was the Reporter trying to achieve...

98
00:06:24,954 --> 00:06:27,689
by calling an embargo?

99
00:06:27,690 --> 00:06:30,725
(Reporter rules)

100
00:06:30,726 --> 00:06:32,360
If there were a spy...

101
00:06:32,361 --> 00:06:35,497
on the verge of elimination,

102
00:06:35,498 --> 00:06:36,965
who would they want gone the most?

103
00:06:36,966 --> 00:06:40,135
They accessed the Reporter's code.

104
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:42,504
The rest of the residents put...

105
00:06:42,505 --> 00:06:44,406
their complete trust in each other...

106
00:06:44,407 --> 00:06:46,341
and agreed...

107
00:06:46,342 --> 00:06:49,378
to burn their attack tokens
so everyone would be equal.

108
00:06:49,379 --> 00:06:52,881
The spy couldn't use
the code they cracked,

109
00:06:52,882 --> 00:06:54,116
so nobody got eliminated.

110
00:06:54,117 --> 00:06:56,251
But that's not how the real world works.

111
00:06:56,252 --> 00:06:58,020
If everyone could just share information,

112
00:06:58,021 --> 00:07:00,155
never attack each other,
and live happily ever after,

113
00:07:00,156 --> 00:07:01,323
that would be a utopia.

114
00:07:01,578 --> 00:07:02,491
But is that even realistic?

115
00:07:02,492 --> 00:07:04,161
I couldn't stop questioning...

116
00:07:04,162 --> 00:07:05,561
whether that kind of ideal society
could ever work.

117
00:07:05,562 --> 00:07:08,597
I wanted to put that question out there.

118
00:07:08,598 --> 00:07:11,033
Can the community...

119
00:07:11,568 --> 00:07:15,271
they're dreaming of actually survive?

120
00:07:15,505 --> 00:07:17,906
I wanted to push the envelope...

121
00:07:18,074 --> 00:07:20,275
just a bit further.

122
00:07:20,276 --> 00:07:22,411
I was hoping the spy would stir
things up...

123
00:07:22,412 --> 00:07:24,579
and stay in the game a little longer.

124
00:07:24,781 --> 00:07:25,714
That's why I did it.

125
00:07:26,082 --> 00:07:29,017
I cried because I knew
how cruel I was being.

126
00:07:29,018 --> 00:07:32,554
"Spy, I bought you some time."

127
00:07:32,555 --> 00:07:34,890
"I called this embargo..."

128
00:07:34,891 --> 00:07:37,559
"to keep you alive." That was
the real point of the embargo for me.

129
00:07:38,595 --> 00:07:40,396
I thought about it.

130
00:07:40,397 --> 00:07:42,665
Today...

131
00:07:42,666 --> 00:07:46,235
the 12 of us teamed up
and made this happen.

132
00:07:46,236 --> 00:07:48,837
We instructed the Reporter to ask,

133
00:07:48,838 --> 00:07:50,806
"Who is the spy?"

134
00:07:51,474 --> 00:07:55,010
We're bound to find out eventually.

135
00:07:55,011 --> 00:07:57,479
And then the spy gets eliminated...

136
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:58,780
without ever making a move.

137
00:07:58,781 --> 00:08:01,951
So I don't get why she was crying.

138
00:08:01,952 --> 00:08:05,053
To balance the scales,

139
00:08:05,622 --> 00:08:08,391
the game might force
the Reporter to choose...

140
00:08:08,392 --> 00:08:10,025
between both sides.

141
00:08:11,227 --> 00:08:14,296
If the residents win,
she takes the prize money.

142
00:08:15,064 --> 00:08:19,167
If she sides with the spy, they split it.

143
00:08:20,235 --> 00:08:23,205
- So looking at it from her shoes,
- If that gives her an edge...

144
00:08:23,206 --> 00:08:25,740
she has to weigh her options.

145
00:08:26,876 --> 00:08:29,010
Especially when she doesn't
know how the rules will change.

146
00:08:30,613 --> 00:08:32,914
If she eliminated the spy right away,

147
00:08:32,939 --> 00:08:35,841
it wouldn't guarantee
her safety or any payout.

148
00:08:36,319 --> 00:08:39,288
She has to decide whether siding
with the spy pays off,

149
00:08:39,289 --> 00:08:41,490
so her reaction makes sense to me.

150
00:08:41,791 --> 00:08:43,392
The media isn't always...

151
00:08:43,393 --> 00:08:46,395
- on the people's side.
- That's true.

152
00:08:46,396 --> 00:08:50,699
It can align with the wrong interests...

153
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:52,367
or get compromised along the way.

154
00:08:54,071 --> 00:08:56,071
- That's the classic media dilemma.
- Exactly.

155
00:09:04,013 --> 00:09:08,316
Honestly, the embargo didn't strike
me as suspicious.

156
00:09:08,317 --> 00:09:10,185
Once she declared it,

157
00:09:10,186 --> 00:09:11,720
there was nothing for us to do...

158
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:14,022
until it was lifted.

159
00:09:14,457 --> 00:09:16,224
I didn't see what the fuss was about.

160
00:09:16,693 --> 00:09:18,861
It meant we didn't have
to stress about it for a while,

161
00:09:18,862 --> 00:09:20,762
but everyone else got super defensive.

162
00:09:20,763 --> 00:09:23,231
They started overthinking every angle.

163
00:09:23,232 --> 00:09:25,901
I was exhausted. It couldn't be helped.

164
00:09:25,902 --> 00:09:27,970
I needed to step away and recharge.

165
00:09:28,771 --> 00:09:30,872
- What was the first question?
- "Who is the spy?"

166
00:09:30,873 --> 00:09:33,875
- And question two?
- "Whose code did the spy view today?"

167
00:09:33,876 --> 00:09:36,878
In that case, the answer had...

168
00:09:36,879 --> 00:09:38,179
to be Nangja.

169
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,181
- Right. Then in that case...
- Which would explain...

170
00:09:40,182 --> 00:09:42,751
why she can't tell us.

171
00:09:42,752 --> 00:09:45,320
But then she would've
been eliminated by now.

172
00:09:45,321 --> 00:09:46,755
I doubt it.

173
00:09:46,756 --> 00:09:50,091
I think Hippo was the one
who checked the code.

174
00:09:51,027 --> 00:09:53,028
Where did Hippo go?

175
00:09:53,029 --> 00:09:55,330
Hippo.

176
00:09:55,598 --> 00:09:58,166
She could've asked for protection.

177
00:09:58,167 --> 00:10:00,936
Nangja just stepped out.

178
00:10:01,671 --> 00:10:03,505
How much did you see before you left?

179
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:06,041
You left after seeing her cry, right?

180
00:10:06,309 --> 00:10:07,142
Yes.

181
00:10:08,511 --> 00:10:10,412
Actually,

182
00:10:11,047 --> 00:10:12,347
a thought just crossed my mind.

183
00:10:12,348 --> 00:10:13,382
- What?
- Like what?

184
00:10:13,383 --> 00:10:15,617
I have a feeling they'll bring
in a new person.

185
00:10:15,985 --> 00:10:17,419
- And that made her cry?
- Another person?

186
00:10:17,854 --> 00:10:20,022
We've had an even split
of six men and six women.

187
00:10:20,423 --> 00:10:24,059
Out of nowhere, it struck me
that they won't keep it even.

188
00:10:24,594 --> 00:10:26,562
Bringing in just one new face...

189
00:10:26,963 --> 00:10:28,330
would throw everything off balance.

190
00:10:31,334 --> 00:10:34,369
Thirteen is a pretty symbolic number.

191
00:10:34,605 --> 00:10:36,605
Are you saying someone off the grid...

192
00:10:36,606 --> 00:10:38,441
has started targeting us?

193
00:10:38,448 --> 00:10:41,316
But in a community like this,

194
00:10:42,111 --> 00:10:44,546
that'd be bizarre.
This isn't a dating show.

195
00:10:44,547 --> 00:10:46,114
Why would they drop in a bombshell?

196
00:10:52,689 --> 00:10:54,122
And about Hippo,

197
00:10:54,123 --> 00:10:57,792
she disappeared for a while,
then came back...

198
00:10:57,793 --> 00:11:01,129
out of nowhere,
claiming the spy could be an outsider.

199
00:11:01,130 --> 00:11:04,399
It came straight out of left field.

200
00:11:04,567 --> 00:11:06,802
When Polar Bear
and I were bouncing around names...

201
00:11:06,803 --> 00:11:08,503
for the spy,

202
00:11:08,504 --> 00:11:12,541
we mentioned that Hippo seemed suspicious.

203
00:11:12,809 --> 00:11:14,610
I can feel the group starting...

204
00:11:14,611 --> 00:11:16,512
to fracture.

205
00:11:16,513 --> 00:11:19,314
If another faction is forming,

206
00:11:19,315 --> 00:11:22,651
it's probably Michael, Dark Knight,
Nangja, Hippo,

207
00:11:22,952 --> 00:11:23,852
and Sugar.

208
00:11:23,853 --> 00:11:27,856
The political landscape is shifting
fast under the surface.

209
00:11:28,024 --> 00:11:30,359
Lines are being drawn,
and alliances are forming.

210
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:32,928
It looks like the labor crew formed...

211
00:11:32,929 --> 00:11:34,563
their own alliance.

212
00:11:34,564 --> 00:11:37,366
Every single person
who voted for Nangja worked...

213
00:11:37,367 --> 00:11:39,534
on the outside labor shift that day.

214
00:11:39,535 --> 00:11:41,670
That was an eye-opener for me.

215
00:11:41,671 --> 00:11:45,173
It made me realize
how political this game really is,

216
00:11:45,341 --> 00:11:47,409
and my survival mode kicked in.

217
00:11:47,877 --> 00:11:50,245
- Let's get some sleep.
- Let's turn in.

218
00:11:55,084 --> 00:11:56,084
Hippo seems sketchy to me, too.

219
00:11:56,085 --> 00:11:58,319
Bringing up the number 13 and all that.

220
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:00,422
- Take a seat.
- My bet's on Hippo.

221
00:12:00,423 --> 00:12:02,357
- Hippo's definitely a suspect.
- Totally.

222
00:12:02,358 --> 00:12:04,359
Nangja, Hippo, and Michael.

223
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:06,094
Nangja and Hippo...

224
00:12:06,295 --> 00:12:07,795
approached me together.

225
00:12:08,197 --> 00:12:10,765
The women who stayed
behind formed their own group.

226
00:12:10,766 --> 00:12:13,802
I had no idea Hippo was like that.
She played her cards really close to her.

227
00:12:13,803 --> 00:12:15,737
Was Michael the one gunning for me?

228
00:12:15,738 --> 00:12:18,774
She vanished for a while,
then suddenly brought up the number 13.

229
00:12:18,775 --> 00:12:20,041
That was...

230
00:12:22,411 --> 00:12:24,779
Let me get this off my chest first.

231
00:12:25,014 --> 00:12:27,549
Right when we got back from work,

232
00:12:27,550 --> 00:12:29,250
Hippo pulled me aside.

233
00:12:29,251 --> 00:12:32,253
She said there was
something she had to tell me...

234
00:12:32,555 --> 00:12:33,855
and walked me all the way
to the back room.

235
00:12:34,290 --> 00:12:37,359
She said she wanted to give me a heads-up.

236
00:12:37,593 --> 00:12:39,761
When I asked what she wanted in return,

237
00:12:40,463 --> 00:12:42,364
she told me she hoped I'd use
my immunity card on her.

238
00:12:43,633 --> 00:12:45,500
- That was her angle.
- That sends chills down my spine.

239
00:12:45,501 --> 00:12:46,301
The nerve of her.

240
00:12:46,302 --> 00:12:47,469
And that was where we left it.

241
00:12:47,470 --> 00:12:50,606
Now that we have a read on the room,

242
00:12:50,607 --> 00:12:52,173
shouldn't we use it to our advantage?

243
00:12:52,174 --> 00:12:55,143
The funny thing is that she's
never approached me once.

244
00:12:55,144 --> 00:12:55,877
Right.

245
00:12:56,179 --> 00:12:57,913
You're not even on her radar.

246
00:12:57,914 --> 00:13:00,315
So she's been making moves
behind the scenes this whole time.

247
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:02,284
She might've formed a feminist group.

248
00:13:03,753 --> 00:13:05,520
She definitely started her own faction.

249
00:13:06,055 --> 00:13:07,022
She seems like a politician.

250
00:13:07,023 --> 00:13:08,056
- Who?
- Hippo.

251
00:13:09,225 --> 00:13:11,226
I've noticed she uses...

252
00:13:11,227 --> 00:13:13,495
a lot of the same words I use.

253
00:13:13,863 --> 00:13:17,032
The kinds of words you'd expect
from a progressive feminist woman.

254
00:13:17,033 --> 00:13:19,334
That really stood out to me,
and it made me uncomfortable.

255
00:13:19,335 --> 00:13:22,104
She uses a lot of political
and social language.

256
00:13:22,105 --> 00:13:24,172
Earlier, when we were talking,

257
00:13:24,173 --> 00:13:27,475
she said she runs a community
of more than 300 people.

258
00:13:27,476 --> 00:13:29,678
She even used the word "community."

259
00:13:29,679 --> 00:13:31,446
If I may highlight my leadership a little,

260
00:13:31,447 --> 00:13:33,882
I actually run a community myself.

261
00:13:33,883 --> 00:13:36,151
Around 300 people have been part
of it so far.

262
00:13:36,152 --> 00:13:39,621
Given the circumstances,
if she's a politician,

263
00:13:40,056 --> 00:13:41,323
she's in a position...

264
00:13:41,958 --> 00:13:44,826
to make political deals, win people over,
and rally them to her side.

265
00:13:45,133 --> 00:13:46,967
Without us realizing it,

266
00:13:47,597 --> 00:13:49,932
they have started getting
on the same page.

267
00:13:50,333 --> 00:13:53,468
I paid close attention
when we were choosing the Reporter.

268
00:13:53,469 --> 00:13:54,637
They all voted for her...

269
00:13:54,638 --> 00:13:56,638
like they were in on it together.

270
00:13:56,663 --> 00:13:57,763
Hippo instigated it.

271
00:13:58,608 --> 00:14:00,842
(The facts are getting mixed up
with misconceptions.)

272
00:14:01,477 --> 00:14:03,311
Genie seems to be testing the waters.

273
00:14:03,312 --> 00:14:04,312
Genie.

274
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:06,982
- Genie is...
- Nangja is with Hippo for sure.

275
00:14:06,983 --> 00:14:09,350
If Nangja starts leveraging
her position as Reporter,

276
00:14:09,351 --> 00:14:10,352
we'll be at a disadvantage.

277
00:14:10,353 --> 00:14:12,186
She'll withhold the information.

278
00:14:13,322 --> 00:14:15,556
Wait. Something suddenly occurred to me.

279
00:14:15,557 --> 00:14:16,557
How much money do you guys have?

280
00:14:16,558 --> 00:14:19,260
If you have zero dollars,
you can attack without worrying.

281
00:14:19,261 --> 00:14:20,194
I have zero dollars.

282
00:14:20,195 --> 00:14:23,031
Starting tomorrow,
let's figure out all our options...

283
00:14:23,032 --> 00:14:24,632
and take Hippo down first.

284
00:14:24,633 --> 00:14:27,134
- Hippo.
- She's easy.

285
00:14:27,135 --> 00:14:29,603
So, let's try eliminating one.

286
00:14:29,604 --> 00:14:30,638
Hippo!

287
00:14:31,274 --> 00:14:32,407
It's war now.

288
00:14:32,808 --> 00:14:35,777
We only have three days left...

289
00:14:35,778 --> 00:14:37,845
to take someone out with an attack token.

290
00:14:40,049 --> 00:14:42,618
We're all split now.

291
00:14:42,619 --> 00:14:43,718
This is fun.

292
00:14:44,253 --> 00:14:45,587
Let's settle one thing.

293
00:14:45,855 --> 00:14:47,956
From now on,

294
00:14:47,957 --> 00:14:49,658
let's just play along with Hippo.

295
00:14:49,659 --> 00:14:50,625
Okay.

296
00:14:50,794 --> 00:14:52,293
Let's just humor Hippo for now.

297
00:14:53,262 --> 00:14:54,062
We play dumb with her.

298
00:14:54,063 --> 00:14:56,497
We should start considering
her a suspect now.

299
00:14:56,498 --> 00:14:58,132
Don't make it look too obvious.

300
00:14:58,133 --> 00:14:59,334
This is also important.

301
00:14:59,335 --> 00:15:01,436
We should bring Benjamin over.

302
00:15:01,704 --> 00:15:02,771
For sure.

303
00:15:02,772 --> 00:15:05,439
He's one of the key players, like Ted.

304
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:07,375
Ted goes along with it if it's reasonable.

305
00:15:07,376 --> 00:15:08,877
- Dark Knight too.
- Genie...

306
00:15:10,179 --> 00:15:11,312
(Tensions Under the Roof)

307
00:15:11,615 --> 00:15:13,748
(The Community)

308
00:15:14,450 --> 00:15:17,719
(The Emergence of a New Alliance)

309
00:15:18,387 --> 00:15:19,420
You're right.

310
00:15:19,421 --> 00:15:20,822
(Day Four, morning)

311
00:15:21,257 --> 00:15:22,324
Yesterday...

312
00:15:22,325 --> 00:15:23,158
(Gray and Superman are up first.)

313
00:15:23,159 --> 00:15:25,660
was a wake-up call.

314
00:15:26,462 --> 00:15:28,863
I'm not here to debate.

315
00:15:29,631 --> 00:15:31,032
I'm here to survive.

316
00:15:32,935 --> 00:15:34,102
True.

317
00:15:34,103 --> 00:15:36,971
Last night, I suddenly had...

318
00:15:36,972 --> 00:15:38,740
- this survival instinct kick in.
- I know.

319
00:15:38,741 --> 00:15:40,141
It put me on high alert.

320
00:15:41,277 --> 00:15:43,745
I have another strategy we could try.

321
00:15:44,013 --> 00:15:46,848
If all four of us attack them,
it might give us away.

322
00:15:47,083 --> 00:15:48,651
What if just one of us goes after...

323
00:15:48,652 --> 00:15:51,519
one of the players we want to shake up?

324
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:55,257
If they find out it was us,
they'd defend harder.

325
00:15:55,258 --> 00:15:57,959
- So everyone at once?
- We all go in at once.

326
00:15:57,960 --> 00:16:00,429
To increase our chances? Okay.

327
00:16:00,629 --> 00:16:02,730
As we discussed last night,

328
00:16:03,132 --> 00:16:06,835
Polar Bear will keep listening
to whatever Hippo has to say.

329
00:16:07,745 --> 00:16:11,147
And I know Sugar.

330
00:16:11,974 --> 00:16:15,877
Sugar, I wasn't sure at first,
but I think I recognize you now.

331
00:16:16,078 --> 00:16:17,311
Really?

332
00:16:17,747 --> 00:16:20,448
We worked at the same company.

333
00:16:20,449 --> 00:16:21,683
What do you do?

334
00:16:22,484 --> 00:16:25,153
You were taking photos, and I was writing.

335
00:16:27,056 --> 00:16:29,124
I've been writing as a columnist...

336
00:16:29,325 --> 00:16:30,859
for "Maxim" for two years.

337
00:16:31,227 --> 00:16:33,962
There was an event hosted by Maxim Korea,

338
00:16:34,096 --> 00:16:36,564
and Sugar was the emcee.

339
00:16:36,565 --> 00:16:39,033
I had no idea it was her.

340
00:16:39,034 --> 00:16:41,369
She seems a little shy now,

341
00:16:41,636 --> 00:16:43,170
but she was a completely
different person back then.

342
00:16:43,571 --> 00:16:46,942
If that's true,
Hippo and Sugar can't be on the same team.

343
00:16:46,943 --> 00:16:48,009
Their views on gender would be different.

344
00:16:48,010 --> 00:16:49,944
You're right there,

345
00:16:49,945 --> 00:16:52,947
but I think Sugar is focusing...

346
00:16:53,516 --> 00:16:55,116
on surviving.

347
00:16:55,251 --> 00:16:57,953
What if you get close to her...

348
00:16:57,954 --> 00:16:59,721
and say Hippo might be karen3000.

349
00:16:59,722 --> 00:17:02,290
Try to steer the conversation
toward feminism.

350
00:17:02,491 --> 00:17:05,426
- That's a good idea.
- Since she's not a feminist.

351
00:17:05,427 --> 00:17:07,829
Try to feel her out.

352
00:17:07,830 --> 00:17:10,064
Okay. I'll see what she says.

353
00:17:10,499 --> 00:17:13,434
We should pull Dark Knight in soon too.

354
00:17:14,102 --> 00:17:15,770
We should take a different approach.

355
00:17:16,338 --> 00:17:19,140
If I think about "loyalty"
or "brotherhood,"

356
00:17:19,141 --> 00:17:21,041
you might be the perfect person
to approach him.

357
00:17:21,042 --> 00:17:22,109
He's the type of person I like.

358
00:17:22,979 --> 00:17:25,113
- The type of person I like to approach.
- Exactly.

359
00:17:25,114 --> 00:17:27,715
When Michael gave him some ground,

360
00:17:27,716 --> 00:17:29,818
once they came together out of loyalty,

361
00:17:29,819 --> 00:17:32,120
he stuck by him and kept him in the fold.

362
00:17:32,121 --> 00:17:35,156
Like he saw him as a weak link
on the team.

363
00:17:35,157 --> 00:17:37,158
Maybe it comes
from his military background.

364
00:17:37,159 --> 00:17:40,462
You might be the better person
to approach Dark Knight.

365
00:17:40,463 --> 00:17:41,630
- Me?
- Yes.

366
00:17:41,631 --> 00:17:42,764
That's my personal opinion.

367
00:17:46,669 --> 00:17:48,903
- Is everyone still sleeping?
- They're not getting up.

368
00:17:49,271 --> 00:17:50,371
Look how peaceful it is.

369
00:17:51,740 --> 00:17:52,907
Are you all right?

370
00:17:52,908 --> 00:17:55,643
Do I not look fine? Why?

371
00:17:55,644 --> 00:17:57,645
Well, you cried last night.

372
00:18:07,123 --> 00:18:10,092
It was so awkward.
I tried not to make eye contact.

373
00:18:10,093 --> 00:18:13,161
There were plenty of people
I could open up to,

374
00:18:13,162 --> 00:18:16,465
and everything just shifted overnight.

375
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:18,933
Good morning.

376
00:18:18,934 --> 00:18:19,834
Morning.

377
00:18:20,469 --> 00:18:21,803
Good morning.

378
00:18:21,804 --> 00:18:22,771
Hey.

379
00:18:22,772 --> 00:18:23,972
- Did you sleep well?
- Good morning.

380
00:18:23,973 --> 00:18:25,407
- Morning.
- Welcome.

381
00:18:25,741 --> 00:18:26,908
Your outfit...

382
00:18:27,243 --> 00:18:28,710
looks cool.

383
00:18:35,151 --> 00:18:36,485
By the way, Sugar...

384
00:18:38,387 --> 00:18:39,954
- Excuse me.
- Okay.

385
00:18:40,490 --> 00:18:42,990
Dark Knight, do you want...

386
00:18:43,425 --> 00:18:45,393
Dark Knight, do you want some food?

387
00:18:45,394 --> 00:18:46,961
What? Do you want to share this?

388
00:18:47,296 --> 00:18:48,229
Okay.

389
00:18:51,210 --> 00:18:52,366
Did you get any sleep last night?

390
00:18:52,902 --> 00:18:55,337
I tried my best to just stay in bed.

391
00:18:55,771 --> 00:18:58,740
Yesterday was the best time
to have some drinks.

392
00:18:58,765 --> 00:19:00,232
There'll be other opportunities...

393
00:19:00,576 --> 00:19:01,442
later.

394
00:19:02,311 --> 00:19:04,646
As I said earlier,

395
00:19:04,647 --> 00:19:07,715
I've seen Sugar working on the outside,

396
00:19:07,716 --> 00:19:09,318
and she was so different.

397
00:19:09,319 --> 00:19:10,652
- Completely different.
- I'm so curious.

398
00:19:13,088 --> 00:19:14,755
If I had to compare,

399
00:19:14,756 --> 00:19:17,358
she's kind of like Ted...

400
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:20,061
when she's working in her own field.

401
00:19:21,630 --> 00:19:23,197
- That's really cool.
- Am I?

402
00:19:23,966 --> 00:19:26,200
- I don't know what to do with myself.
- I want to see it.

403
00:19:26,569 --> 00:19:28,203
Dark Knight really grew on me.

404
00:19:28,537 --> 00:19:31,004
- At first, I was scared.
- Really?

405
00:19:31,005 --> 00:19:33,774
I'm kind of intimidated by guys...

406
00:19:33,775 --> 00:19:35,610
who look so masculine.

407
00:19:36,912 --> 00:19:39,747
I get intimidated easily
by that kind of look.

408
00:19:39,748 --> 00:19:41,382
He's more masculine than me.

409
00:19:41,383 --> 00:19:42,750
Oh, come on. You're way more masculine.

410
00:19:42,751 --> 00:19:45,019
I've always been more comfortable
around women since I was young.

411
00:19:47,189 --> 00:19:50,324
- I feel more comfortable around men.
- Is that so?

412
00:19:56,131 --> 00:19:57,031
And...

413
00:19:57,866 --> 00:19:59,467
- Hello.
- Your eyes look so puffy.

414
00:19:59,468 --> 00:20:00,401
(Michael is up.)

415
00:20:00,402 --> 00:20:01,436
- What?
- Your eyes are puffy.

416
00:20:01,437 --> 00:20:02,470
They are.

417
00:20:03,906 --> 00:20:05,006
Michael, do you have a cigarette?

418
00:20:05,474 --> 00:20:07,602
- Do you want to have a smoke?
- Sounds good.

419
00:20:07,603 --> 00:20:08,776
Let's have a smoke.

420
00:20:12,881 --> 00:20:14,281
Is there anything you want to say to me?

421
00:20:14,617 --> 00:20:18,186
What exactly are you offering?
An alliance?

422
00:20:18,621 --> 00:20:20,255
- I just want to...
- I just want to see...

423
00:20:20,789 --> 00:20:22,623
if it's worth betting on.

424
00:20:22,624 --> 00:20:24,392
If there's a spy,

425
00:20:24,393 --> 00:20:26,461
they have to think...

426
00:20:26,462 --> 00:20:27,996
- about why I put up an embargo.
- Right.

427
00:20:27,997 --> 00:20:30,231
I believe the spy is
smart enough to do that.

428
00:20:30,232 --> 00:20:32,400
I thought they might have something
to say to me.

429
00:20:32,401 --> 00:20:33,401
Is that so?

430
00:20:34,003 --> 00:20:37,405
I felt like he made it
way too obvious that he was the spy.

431
00:20:37,406 --> 00:20:39,574
We all picked up on it the day before.

432
00:20:39,575 --> 00:20:41,876
"There must be a reason
why Michael is acting like that."

433
00:20:41,877 --> 00:20:43,578
"He can just chill.
Why is he doing all that?"

434
00:20:43,579 --> 00:20:46,547
He pretty much gave himself away
as the spy.

435
00:20:46,548 --> 00:20:48,449
I couldn't find anything suspicious...

436
00:20:48,450 --> 00:20:51,219
about anyone other than Michael.

437
00:20:51,220 --> 00:20:53,755
And by putting up an embargo,

438
00:20:53,756 --> 00:20:57,325
I left everyone wondering
what was going on and set Michael free.

439
00:20:57,326 --> 00:21:00,662
He probably felt like the spotlight
and pressure were...

440
00:21:00,663 --> 00:21:02,197
no longer all on him.

441
00:21:02,198 --> 00:21:03,331
I gave him room to make his move.

442
00:21:03,332 --> 00:21:05,633
But nothing happened,

443
00:21:05,634 --> 00:21:08,936
so I went to him because I wanted
to hear his side of the story.

444
00:21:09,371 --> 00:21:10,938
I have no idea what's going on.

445
00:21:10,939 --> 00:21:12,274
If I could ask you one thing,

446
00:21:12,275 --> 00:21:15,876
can you just tell me a little bit more
about what's going on?

447
00:21:16,145 --> 00:21:19,047
You said the spy isn't allowed
to reveal their identity.

448
00:21:19,281 --> 00:21:21,048
No. That was just my guess.

449
00:21:21,049 --> 00:21:23,517
That's why I wondered if that's
why you couldn't say anything.

450
00:21:23,853 --> 00:21:27,855
I have no idea what the spy wants
or who they are.

451
00:21:28,390 --> 00:21:31,559
I was 100 percent sure
he was the spy yesterday.

452
00:21:32,194 --> 00:21:33,795
"Does that mean he's not?"

453
00:21:33,796 --> 00:21:36,697
She pulled me aside and said,

454
00:21:36,965 --> 00:21:38,399
"Can you guess why I wanted to see you?"

455
00:21:39,101 --> 00:21:39,967
She just said that.

456
00:21:40,202 --> 00:21:43,471
Why?

457
00:21:43,706 --> 00:21:46,141
"Do you want to turn the game around?"

458
00:21:46,142 --> 00:21:48,743
Give me something to work with.

459
00:21:49,178 --> 00:21:50,713
I need some information...

460
00:21:50,714 --> 00:21:52,347
before I can decide...

461
00:21:52,348 --> 00:21:56,284
whether this is even worth turning around
or making a move on.

462
00:21:57,386 --> 00:21:59,220
- Can I ask you one more thing?
- Yes.

463
00:21:59,555 --> 00:22:01,189
What do you think about Benjamin?

464
00:22:01,323 --> 00:22:03,558
He's great.

465
00:22:04,827 --> 00:22:06,194
Benjamin? Fine.

466
00:22:06,762 --> 00:22:08,996
They keep talking to each other
in English like they're pretty close.

467
00:22:08,997 --> 00:22:10,698
Is there someone else pulling the strings
behind the scenes?

468
00:22:10,699 --> 00:22:13,468
I think Benjamin is a strategist.

469
00:22:13,469 --> 00:22:16,003
He never gives anything away,
and he's nice to everyone.

470
00:22:16,004 --> 00:22:19,373
He has a really good image.
On top of that, he's clearly capable.

471
00:22:20,008 --> 00:22:21,642
If there is a spy,

472
00:22:22,010 --> 00:22:24,311
he's by far the most likely one.

473
00:22:30,786 --> 00:22:33,821
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

474
00:22:34,423 --> 00:22:37,358
Based on last night's favorability vote,

475
00:22:37,359 --> 00:22:40,595
the three candidates for today's leader
have been decided.

476
00:22:41,130 --> 00:22:42,664
Here are today's three leader candidates.

477
00:22:43,665 --> 00:22:46,533
Go Ae Shin.

478
00:22:46,534 --> 00:22:50,037
(Day Four leader candidate: Go Ae Shin)

479
00:22:50,038 --> 00:22:52,273
Genie.

480
00:22:53,375 --> 00:22:55,517
Hippo.

481
00:22:55,518 --> 00:22:57,111
(Day Four leader candidates:
Go Ae Shin, Genie, Hippo)

482
00:22:57,846 --> 00:22:59,980
Unlike the first two days,

483
00:23:00,349 --> 00:23:03,484
I had a very political reason
for voting for Go Ae Shin.

484
00:23:03,719 --> 00:23:06,054
She was the chef,

485
00:23:06,055 --> 00:23:08,122
and I worked as her sous-chef.

486
00:23:08,190 --> 00:23:12,126
I deliberately said certain things
to the residents...

487
00:23:12,127 --> 00:23:15,563
to steer their votes toward her.

488
00:23:16,165 --> 00:23:17,398
If you enjoyed the food,

489
00:23:17,399 --> 00:23:19,601
please give her your favorability votes.

490
00:23:20,068 --> 00:23:22,970
(Superman saw an opening
and used it to campaign.)

491
00:23:24,573 --> 00:23:26,007
(Last night during the favorability vote)

492
00:23:26,008 --> 00:23:26,908
Like this!

493
00:23:27,009 --> 00:23:28,710
Go Ae Shin, since she cooked.

494
00:23:28,711 --> 00:23:29,744
Go Ae Shin.

495
00:23:29,745 --> 00:23:33,581
(It put Go Ae Shin up for leader.)

496
00:23:34,716 --> 00:23:35,516
(Go Ae Shin)

497
00:23:35,752 --> 00:23:38,619
I thought that if I got
her enough votes...

498
00:23:38,887 --> 00:23:40,654
to become a candidate the next day,

499
00:23:40,856 --> 00:23:43,591
I could get
one of our allies elected as leader.

500
00:23:44,326 --> 00:23:47,328
(Day Four leader candidates:
Go Ae Shin, Genie, Hippo)

501
00:23:48,263 --> 00:23:51,098
(Day Three leader's term ends.)

502
00:23:55,971 --> 00:23:58,406
Once per day,

503
00:23:58,740 --> 00:24:02,142
the spy may view
one resident's full ideology code.

504
00:24:02,911 --> 00:24:05,079
Please choose the resident
you would like to view.

505
00:24:09,918 --> 00:24:12,086
Being a spy is tough.

506
00:24:16,558 --> 00:24:20,094
We've confirmed that there is a spy.

507
00:24:20,629 --> 00:24:24,732
(Michael kept emphasizing
that there was a spy.)

508
00:24:28,237 --> 00:24:29,137
Michael.

509
00:24:30,973 --> 00:24:33,675
(Michael: right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

510
00:24:34,576 --> 00:24:35,910
As I expected.

511
00:24:36,311 --> 00:24:37,077
Thanks.

512
00:24:43,452 --> 00:24:45,219
I hope Michael stops talking to me
in English.

513
00:24:45,220 --> 00:24:46,921
He keeps stirring things up.

514
00:24:47,023 --> 00:24:48,990
I don't really mind,

515
00:24:48,991 --> 00:24:51,625
but now that I'm in a pretty bad spot,

516
00:24:51,626 --> 00:24:54,796
I looked into his code
so I can take him out whenever I need to.

517
00:24:55,631 --> 00:24:58,032
Let's hear the candidates'
campaign promises...

518
00:24:58,033 --> 00:25:00,034
one by one.

519
00:25:00,903 --> 00:25:03,204
Let's start with Genie,
who's been leader before.

520
00:25:04,206 --> 00:25:07,609
I'm a little torn right now.

521
00:25:07,610 --> 00:25:09,210
Given where things stand,

522
00:25:09,378 --> 00:25:11,680
is it better...

523
00:25:11,681 --> 00:25:14,248
to have more people get immunity cards?

524
00:25:14,249 --> 00:25:17,552
I'll think a little more
about whether I actually want...

525
00:25:17,553 --> 00:25:18,987
to be a candidate.

526
00:25:20,155 --> 00:25:21,989
On the other hand,

527
00:25:22,457 --> 00:25:25,659
she already has an immunity card.

528
00:25:26,028 --> 00:25:29,297
With a spy among the 12 of us,

529
00:25:29,298 --> 00:25:31,499
if she becomes the leader again,

530
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:35,002
that means one less chance...

531
00:25:35,003 --> 00:25:37,672
for the spy to get immunity.

532
00:25:39,141 --> 00:25:40,775
I've never been a leader,

533
00:25:40,776 --> 00:25:43,044
so I don't have to worry about that.

534
00:25:43,345 --> 00:25:45,780
I went out and worked yesterday,

535
00:25:45,781 --> 00:25:48,582
and it gave me a lot more confidence
about making money.

536
00:25:49,084 --> 00:25:51,919
We've done two income activities.

537
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:54,823
I'm good at learning things quickly,
mastering them,

538
00:25:54,824 --> 00:25:57,991
and then sharing what I learn
with other people.

539
00:25:58,062 --> 00:26:00,194
So I think I can make good money for us.

540
00:26:01,496 --> 00:26:03,096
- That's my campaign promise.
- That's important.

541
00:26:03,097 --> 00:26:05,032
That's crucial.

542
00:26:05,333 --> 00:26:06,934
I didn't expect...

543
00:26:06,935 --> 00:26:09,204
to become a candidate today.

544
00:26:09,205 --> 00:26:11,405
Now that I am, I'm getting nervous.

545
00:26:11,406 --> 00:26:14,141
- Right. It makes you nervous.
- Let me make this clear.

546
00:26:14,142 --> 00:26:16,944
You can serve
as both the leader and the chef.

547
00:26:18,046 --> 00:26:19,747
I also think that should be a given.

548
00:26:20,449 --> 00:26:22,049
- Then I'm with her!
- I like that.

549
00:26:23,752 --> 00:26:26,353
First, loyalty has always been...

550
00:26:26,354 --> 00:26:28,455
my keyword in life.

551
00:26:28,796 --> 00:26:31,398
- Loyalty!
- I'm not as feminine as I seem,

552
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:33,928
and I'm confident
when it comes to loyalty.

553
00:26:33,929 --> 00:26:36,998
I want "loyalty" to be our keyword today.

554
00:26:36,999 --> 00:26:38,399
I think it can help...

555
00:26:38,400 --> 00:26:40,635
us come together
and work better as a team.

556
00:26:40,636 --> 00:26:41,669
That word stuck with him.

557
00:26:41,970 --> 00:26:43,071
- Loyalty!
- Loyalty!

558
00:26:43,939 --> 00:26:45,573
I want to focus on loyalty today.

559
00:26:45,574 --> 00:26:47,708
Second, I think I can step up more...

560
00:26:47,709 --> 00:26:51,045
with the cooking and put out good food.

561
00:26:54,483 --> 00:26:56,384
Lastly, the leader gets an immunity card.

562
00:26:56,385 --> 00:26:58,786
If I received an immunity card...

563
00:26:59,021 --> 00:27:01,089
and the person who gets
eliminated today...

564
00:27:01,456 --> 00:27:03,157
came to ask me for it,

565
00:27:03,158 --> 00:27:06,427
I'd be willing to give it to them.

566
00:27:06,428 --> 00:27:07,728
My immunity card.

567
00:27:08,497 --> 00:27:10,998
- She's loyal.
- She's indeed loyal.

568
00:27:10,999 --> 00:27:11,966
That's true loyalty.

569
00:27:12,834 --> 00:27:15,035
She's someone I trusted
and had talked to a lot,

570
00:27:15,036 --> 00:27:16,370
so I wanted to make her the leader.

571
00:27:16,371 --> 00:27:19,173
We'd talked a lot
and developed a solid level of trust,

572
00:27:19,174 --> 00:27:21,642
so I felt like the next leader...

573
00:27:21,643 --> 00:27:24,778
should come from within our group.

574
00:27:25,546 --> 00:27:28,282
I felt it was in our best interest...

575
00:27:28,283 --> 00:27:31,252
to secure an immunity card for our side.

576
00:27:31,253 --> 00:27:33,721
The immunity card would be...

577
00:27:33,722 --> 00:27:35,956
in the hands of someone...

578
00:27:35,957 --> 00:27:38,159
who shares our goal
of keeping our community safe.

579
00:27:38,393 --> 00:27:40,361
I voted for Go Ae Shin.

580
00:27:40,362 --> 00:27:42,062
- Go Ae Shin.
- Go Ae Shin.

581
00:27:42,063 --> 00:27:43,897
I'm counting on you to feed us well.

582
00:27:43,898 --> 00:27:45,065
The woman with four points.

583
00:27:45,066 --> 00:27:46,467
Ae Shin, I'm counting on you.

584
00:27:48,570 --> 00:27:49,737
Of course, it's me!

585
00:27:50,072 --> 00:27:51,706
I only have four points.

586
00:27:52,874 --> 00:27:53,974
To be honest,

587
00:27:54,810 --> 00:27:57,979
I promised to transfer my immunity card,

588
00:27:58,346 --> 00:28:00,280
but I'll use it on myself.

589
00:28:01,750 --> 00:28:03,217
Goodbye.

590
00:28:03,685 --> 00:28:06,320
(Today's leader announcement)

591
00:28:07,522 --> 00:28:09,756
The new leader
of the Community House is...

592
00:28:11,693 --> 00:28:14,962
Go Ae Shin.

593
00:28:14,963 --> 00:28:16,130
(Day Four leader, Go Ah Shin)

594
00:28:19,401 --> 00:28:21,836
- All the way from chef to leader.
- I'll be back.

595
00:28:22,471 --> 00:28:23,504
What a perfect arc.

596
00:28:24,139 --> 00:28:25,973
- It's like a drama.
- It is.

597
00:28:32,614 --> 00:28:35,116
(Go Ae Shin: left, feminism,
upper-middle, conservative)

598
00:28:39,889 --> 00:28:41,188
The moment...

599
00:28:41,189 --> 00:28:42,890
I opened the box...

600
00:28:42,891 --> 00:28:44,659
and saw the beautiful,
golden immunity card,

601
00:28:44,660 --> 00:28:46,527
I felt a huge sense of relief.

602
00:28:46,528 --> 00:28:48,729
I realized how much this meant to me.

603
00:28:49,465 --> 00:28:52,566
I'm so happy. Thank you.

604
00:28:56,938 --> 00:28:59,006
- Congratulations, chef.
- Thank you.

605
00:28:59,441 --> 00:29:01,375
I'm feeling a little pressure
about cooking today.

606
00:29:01,376 --> 00:29:03,043
You even get to choose the ingredients.

607
00:29:03,044 --> 00:29:03,878
That's true.

608
00:29:03,879 --> 00:29:05,746
That's basically unlimited power.

609
00:29:07,482 --> 00:29:09,483
(The announcement will now begin.)

610
00:29:09,484 --> 00:29:12,052
There is an important announcement.

611
00:29:12,487 --> 00:29:13,921
What is it now?

612
00:29:16,792 --> 00:29:18,459
The daily leader system...

613
00:29:18,660 --> 00:29:20,961
will end after today, Day Four.

614
00:29:21,530 --> 00:29:22,330
What?

615
00:29:26,902 --> 00:29:29,637
Starting on Day Six and lasting
until the end of your stay,

616
00:29:29,971 --> 00:29:31,672
a single leader...

617
00:29:31,673 --> 00:29:33,974
will take power
for the remainder of your stay.

618
00:29:36,011 --> 00:29:36,944
What does that mean?

619
00:29:38,580 --> 00:29:39,613
Starting on Day Six?

620
00:29:39,815 --> 00:29:40,882
Today's Day Four, right?

621
00:29:41,383 --> 00:29:42,917
- From Day Six?
- Please register two candidates...

622
00:29:42,918 --> 00:29:46,253
for the Lifetime Leader
by the end of today.

623
00:29:48,123 --> 00:29:49,290
By the end of today?

624
00:29:49,291 --> 00:29:51,025
Any resident who wishes to run...

625
00:29:51,092 --> 00:29:55,162
must receive endorsements
from at least four other residents,

626
00:29:55,163 --> 00:29:57,831
not including themselves,
to qualify as a candidate.

627
00:29:58,834 --> 00:30:03,171
Each resident may endorse
only one candidate.

628
00:30:03,705 --> 00:30:05,673
To officially endorse a candidate,

629
00:30:06,208 --> 00:30:08,009
please visit the Community Center...

630
00:30:08,543 --> 00:30:09,910
and say, "I'd like to declare my support."

631
00:30:09,911 --> 00:30:11,345
I'd like to declare my support.

632
00:30:11,346 --> 00:30:14,414
- I'd like to declare my support.
- I'd like to declare my support.

633
00:30:14,415 --> 00:30:16,184
After making your declaration,

634
00:30:16,185 --> 00:30:19,386
submit the name
of the candidate you're endorsing.

635
00:30:19,855 --> 00:30:23,090
Endorsement declarations will open
after income activities conclude...

636
00:30:23,325 --> 00:30:26,027
and remain open
until the Community Center closes.

637
00:30:26,361 --> 00:30:28,095
Once submitted,

638
00:30:28,263 --> 00:30:32,433
an endorsement cannot be
withdrawn or changed.

639
00:30:32,668 --> 00:30:35,536
Tomorrow will be
the official campaign period...

640
00:30:35,537 --> 00:30:37,504
for both candidates.

641
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:41,475
The vote will take place on Day Six.

642
00:30:41,877 --> 00:30:46,013
We wish you all
a fair and honorable election.

643
00:30:46,014 --> 00:30:48,617
- "Honorable?"
- This concludes the announcement...

644
00:30:48,618 --> 00:30:50,017
- from the Community Center.
- That gets on my nerves.

645
00:30:50,018 --> 00:30:53,154
So our daily routine ends today.

646
00:30:53,155 --> 00:30:56,224
Looks like tomorrow is going to be
all about campaigning...

647
00:30:56,225 --> 00:30:57,425
and the election.

648
00:30:57,426 --> 00:30:58,893
Routines go out the window today.

649
00:30:59,427 --> 00:31:01,762
Isn't this basically
a presidential election?

650
00:31:02,765 --> 00:31:05,433
Tomorrow's the hearing,
and the vote is on Day Six.

651
00:31:05,434 --> 00:31:06,567
"The hearing."

652
00:31:06,968 --> 00:31:07,857
This should be good.

653
00:31:07,881 --> 00:31:09,702
We'll have the two candidates
take the floor.

654
00:31:09,703 --> 00:31:11,972
And we can make them debate all day.

655
00:31:11,973 --> 00:31:13,540
Got it. That makes sense.

656
00:31:13,842 --> 00:31:16,210
It's a permanent post,
meaning they hold power until the end.

657
00:31:16,211 --> 00:31:18,646
That's massive leverage.

658
00:31:18,980 --> 00:31:20,847
Why are we even electing
a lifetime leader?

659
00:31:20,992 --> 00:31:22,424
What guarantee do we have...

660
00:31:22,449 --> 00:31:24,418
that unchecked power
won't breed corruption?

661
00:31:24,712 --> 00:31:28,114
Which is why we have to do
whatever it takes...

662
00:31:28,456 --> 00:31:30,424
to install someone
the peace alliance trusts.

663
00:31:30,725 --> 00:31:32,928
Dictatorship, martial law,
oppression, tyranny.

664
00:31:32,929 --> 00:31:35,029
Those are the words that come to mind.

665
00:31:35,030 --> 00:31:36,686
"If we're all going
to make it out together,"

666
00:31:36,710 --> 00:31:37,364
"you do as I say."

667
00:31:37,365 --> 00:31:39,233
They could even start muzzling the press.

668
00:31:39,234 --> 00:31:42,436
What kind of authority
will the Lifetime Leader actually have?

669
00:31:42,704 --> 00:31:44,405
Is this where...

670
00:31:44,406 --> 00:31:46,407
the all-out war begins?

671
00:31:47,075 --> 00:31:49,844
We don't know what privileges
come with the role yet.

672
00:31:50,712 --> 00:31:51,712
Right.

673
00:31:51,913 --> 00:31:53,314
Could there be a way to impeach them?

674
00:31:53,551 --> 00:31:54,681
- Impeachment?
- There might be.

675
00:31:54,682 --> 00:31:56,550
It's possible. We could vote them out.

676
00:31:56,551 --> 00:31:58,250
If that's an option,

677
00:31:58,251 --> 00:31:59,986
and a non-spy takes the seat,

678
00:31:59,987 --> 00:32:01,555
the spies will definitely
try to tear them down.

679
00:32:01,556 --> 00:32:03,089
Fair point.

680
00:32:03,458 --> 00:32:05,726
Then again, the spy could win the seat.

681
00:32:05,894 --> 00:32:07,194
That could easily happen.

682
00:32:07,395 --> 00:32:09,163
In fact, the odds are pretty high.

683
00:32:09,164 --> 00:32:12,000
What if the spy has just been
biding their time for this?

684
00:32:12,434 --> 00:32:15,469
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

685
00:32:15,904 --> 00:32:20,041
Today's income activity is
"Trek Across the Globe."

686
00:32:20,042 --> 00:32:20,941
Oh, wow.

687
00:32:23,078 --> 00:32:24,045
We picked the right leader.

688
00:32:24,046 --> 00:32:25,780
That sounds physically exhausting.

689
00:32:26,247 --> 00:32:28,682
Or it could be map-reading.

690
00:32:28,683 --> 00:32:32,185
The maximum prize pool
for today's income activity...

691
00:32:35,557 --> 00:32:37,691
is 100,000 dollars.

692
00:32:39,594 --> 00:32:40,494
No way.

693
00:32:40,862 --> 00:32:42,230
Did they say 100,000 dollars? Today?

694
00:32:43,298 --> 00:32:44,398
Today is 100,000 dollars?

695
00:32:46,301 --> 00:32:49,036
It's 100,000 dollars?
That blows every previous payout...

696
00:32:49,037 --> 00:32:50,371
completely out of the water.

697
00:32:50,472 --> 00:32:52,372
That's hard to pass up.

698
00:32:54,709 --> 00:32:56,276
"Trek Across the Globe."

699
00:32:56,778 --> 00:32:58,545
Someone with solid navigational skills.

700
00:32:58,546 --> 00:33:00,181
I think I'd actually do pretty well...

701
00:33:00,182 --> 00:33:01,749
- with that.
- Great. You're locked in.

702
00:33:01,950 --> 00:33:05,219
We definitely need someone
well-versed in history.

703
00:33:05,220 --> 00:33:08,355
Agreed. Someone sharp
on world history, foreign affairs,

704
00:33:08,356 --> 00:33:09,623
and geography.

705
00:33:11,726 --> 00:33:15,228
Could it involve foreign languages,
given the title "Trek Across the Globe"?

706
00:33:15,229 --> 00:33:16,763
Foreign languages?

707
00:33:16,764 --> 00:33:20,267
We should probably factor that in,
just in case.

708
00:33:20,568 --> 00:33:22,669
Nangja, aren't you pretty plugged
into international affairs?

709
00:33:23,104 --> 00:33:24,139
International affairs?

710
00:33:24,639 --> 00:33:27,140
- I used to work in that field, yeah.
- There you go.

711
00:33:27,141 --> 00:33:29,409
- I nominate Nangja.
- I second Nangja.

712
00:33:29,410 --> 00:33:31,745
Plus, she hasn't been out
on an income run yet.

713
00:33:31,746 --> 00:33:33,581
That's true!

714
00:33:33,582 --> 00:33:36,183
If you're all good with me going,
even with the Reporter role.

715
00:33:36,184 --> 00:33:38,151
- Absolutely.
- That actually works out nicely.

716
00:33:38,152 --> 00:33:39,854
The living room seems pretty laid-back.

717
00:33:40,922 --> 00:33:43,924
Our former leader mentioned
wanting to rest today.

718
00:33:43,925 --> 00:33:46,260
- Benjamin? All right.
- Gray too.

719
00:33:46,261 --> 00:33:48,262
Gray said he wants
to sit this one out as well.

720
00:33:48,530 --> 00:33:51,098
- Sugar too.
- Sugar? Got it.

721
00:33:51,099 --> 00:33:52,466
Keep that in mind when picking.

722
00:33:54,536 --> 00:33:56,771
I'm not sure if I should just
sit this one out as well.

723
00:33:57,572 --> 00:33:59,273
Come on, join us.

724
00:33:59,274 --> 00:34:00,140
You really want me to?

725
00:34:00,141 --> 00:34:02,794
- I was kind of leaning toward...
- But yesterday...

726
00:34:02,819 --> 00:34:04,511
You campaigned on being great
at this kind of thing.

727
00:34:04,512 --> 00:34:05,445
You can't tap out now.

728
00:34:05,446 --> 00:34:07,093
(Benjamin, Sugar, and Gray sit out.)

729
00:34:07,117 --> 00:34:08,248
(They urge Hippo to go.)

730
00:34:08,383 --> 00:34:09,651
Michael would be great too.

731
00:34:09,652 --> 00:34:11,151
I really want to bring him along.

732
00:34:11,419 --> 00:34:13,087
I'm going to take a breather inside today.

733
00:34:13,255 --> 00:34:14,854
Yesterday took a lot out of me.

734
00:34:14,855 --> 00:34:17,324
Michael would crush it, though.

735
00:34:17,325 --> 00:34:18,258
(Michael: a prime recruitment target)

736
00:34:18,326 --> 00:34:21,028
Let me announce today's roster.

737
00:34:21,496 --> 00:34:24,631
Today's challenge is
"Trek Across the Globe."

738
00:34:24,833 --> 00:34:28,235
I'm good with brute physical labor,
so I'll go.

739
00:34:28,603 --> 00:34:30,737
During our very first income run,

740
00:34:30,738 --> 00:34:32,206
Superman showed really solid judgment.

741
00:34:32,207 --> 00:34:33,140
So he's on the team.

742
00:34:33,141 --> 00:34:37,344
Also, Nangja has a strong background
in foreign languages and global history.

743
00:34:37,345 --> 00:34:41,014
Hippo mentioned having good momentum
from yesterday, so she's in.

744
00:34:41,216 --> 00:34:42,984
And Ted.

745
00:34:43,218 --> 00:34:44,952
I was hoping...

746
00:34:44,953 --> 00:34:47,521
you'd back me up as my second-in-command.

747
00:34:47,522 --> 00:34:48,756
That actually fits you well.

748
00:34:49,290 --> 00:34:51,091
- So...
- Say no more.

749
00:34:52,577 --> 00:34:54,461
I'm naming you today's second-in-command.

750
00:34:54,462 --> 00:34:56,130
Second-in-command, love it!

751
00:34:56,131 --> 00:34:58,432
And Genie, who pulled
a lot of weight yesterday.

752
00:34:58,833 --> 00:35:00,801
- That's a rock-solid squad.
- What do you think?

753
00:35:00,802 --> 00:35:02,702
I'm glad to have Genie on board.

754
00:35:03,538 --> 00:35:04,938
This lineup feels super dependable.

755
00:35:04,939 --> 00:35:05,939
(The Day Four income crew)

756
00:35:06,641 --> 00:35:08,076
- Let's kill it out there.
- Let's do this.

757
00:35:16,351 --> 00:35:18,585
First off, this played out great.

758
00:35:18,586 --> 00:35:20,421
We managed to take the people we needed...

759
00:35:20,688 --> 00:35:22,423
and leave behind the ones...

760
00:35:22,448 --> 00:35:24,190
- we still have to sway.
- Nice work.

761
00:35:24,792 --> 00:35:26,760
I spoke with Go Ae Shin.

762
00:35:27,991 --> 00:35:29,730
(The four-person alliance's game plan)

763
00:35:29,731 --> 00:35:32,366
Even if one of us gets nominated tomorrow,

764
00:35:32,367 --> 00:35:34,301
our odds of actually winning the vote
are pretty slim.

765
00:35:34,454 --> 00:35:35,646
If even one of us makes the ballot,

766
00:35:35,670 --> 00:35:37,605
we have to throw all our weight
behind them.

767
00:35:38,106 --> 00:35:40,874
(Objective one:
getting Go Ae Shin elected leader)

768
00:35:41,309 --> 00:35:43,543
Getting Go Ae Shin
into the leader seat was huge.

769
00:35:43,878 --> 00:35:46,580
Setting that narrative early on
really paid off.

770
00:35:46,581 --> 00:35:49,115
I kept talking up her cooking
every chance I got.

771
00:35:49,984 --> 00:35:52,218
(Superman steadily laid the groundwork.)

772
00:35:52,587 --> 00:35:55,622
People naturally feel indebted
when someone feeds them,

773
00:35:55,623 --> 00:35:57,724
so I knew I could cash in
on that goodwill eventually.

774
00:35:57,725 --> 00:36:00,494
Just as planned,
we set up a 2-on-1 split today.

775
00:36:00,495 --> 00:36:03,430
That's why when Genie was
thinking of pulling out,

776
00:36:03,431 --> 00:36:06,033
I talked her down by pointing out
that running wouldn't necessarily...

777
00:36:06,034 --> 00:36:06,934
work against her.

778
00:36:07,235 --> 00:36:09,770
(Genie's attempt to drop out...)

779
00:36:09,771 --> 00:36:12,573
(is shut down.)

780
00:36:13,141 --> 00:36:14,808
(Day Two income team: Go Ae Shin,
Superman, Polar Bear, Gray, Benjamin, Ted)

781
00:36:14,809 --> 00:36:16,009
(Day Two stay-behind crew: Genie, Hippo,
Michael, Nangja, Dark Knight, Sugar )

782
00:36:16,010 --> 00:36:20,048
I figured the votes within that circle
would split between Genie and Hippo.

783
00:36:20,224 --> 00:36:22,692
Our four votes were locked in
and rock solid.

784
00:36:22,717 --> 00:36:23,884
(Splitting Hippo's voting base)

785
00:36:24,185 --> 00:36:26,887
(Objective two: curating the income crew)

786
00:36:26,888 --> 00:36:30,423
The lifetime leader is
going to hold all the cards.

787
00:36:30,592 --> 00:36:32,626
So we need to win over swing votes...

788
00:36:32,627 --> 00:36:35,796
while keeping a close eye
on where everyone leans.

789
00:36:35,797 --> 00:36:37,965
Go Ae Shin and I head out on the run,

790
00:36:37,966 --> 00:36:41,402
while Polar Bear and Gray stay behind
to take the temperature...

791
00:36:41,403 --> 00:36:42,803
of the house.

792
00:36:42,804 --> 00:36:46,440
Our main priority was
keeping us two apart.

793
00:36:46,741 --> 00:36:50,377
On top of that, I really wanted
to pull Hippo onto our squad.

794
00:36:50,645 --> 00:36:52,879
(Target of interest number one: Hippo)

795
00:36:53,358 --> 00:36:54,981
Come on, join us.

796
00:36:55,175 --> 00:36:58,714
I couldn't risk leaving Hippo behind
with the house crew...

797
00:36:58,747 --> 00:37:00,387
to plot unchecked,

798
00:37:00,388 --> 00:37:02,656
so I insisted on bringing her with us.

799
00:37:02,681 --> 00:37:03,881
(Keeping the biggest wildcard in sight)

800
00:37:04,025 --> 00:37:08,462
It's obvious Michael and Nangja
are running together.

801
00:37:08,463 --> 00:37:09,918
So I stepped in immediately...

802
00:37:09,943 --> 00:37:12,466
and highlighted
her foreign-language background.

803
00:37:12,743 --> 00:37:15,578
(Target of interest number two: Nangja)

804
00:37:15,603 --> 00:37:16,703
I nominate Nangja.

805
00:37:16,704 --> 00:37:19,706
(Putting her directly
in Superman's crosshairs)

806
00:37:20,475 --> 00:37:22,576
Today is Day Six.

807
00:37:22,710 --> 00:37:24,711
One, two, three, four, five, six.

808
00:37:26,648 --> 00:37:29,350
Here's today's field team.

809
00:37:29,351 --> 00:37:32,886
(Day Four income crew: Go Ae Shin,
Superman, Nangja, Hippo, Ted, Genie)

810
00:37:33,189 --> 00:37:36,123
You'll set out for the income challenge
in one hour,

811
00:37:36,691 --> 00:37:39,860
so please get yourselves ready.

812
00:37:40,695 --> 00:37:43,464
Would it be pushing it
if I asked for an iced Americano?

813
00:37:43,465 --> 00:37:45,966
- Count me in.
- Iced Americanos all around.

814
00:37:46,434 --> 00:37:47,401
Leader Go.

815
00:37:47,402 --> 00:37:49,870
Any chance I could get an iced Americano?

816
00:37:49,871 --> 00:37:51,538
- Seven iced Americanos?
- Make it eight.

817
00:37:51,539 --> 00:37:53,573
I'll grab some milk too.

818
00:37:55,953 --> 00:37:58,154
Building up the prize pot is
great and all,

819
00:37:58,179 --> 00:38:01,247
but in a way,
it's just fuel for new conflict.

820
00:38:01,248 --> 00:38:04,050
Because that money buys attack tokens.

821
00:38:04,051 --> 00:38:05,252
Exactly.

822
00:38:05,253 --> 00:38:07,286
(Once the initial attack token is spent,
an extra one costs 1,000 dollars.)

823
00:38:07,287 --> 00:38:09,823
- What now?
- The real game of survival begins now.

824
00:38:10,058 --> 00:38:12,426
Today really rattles me.

825
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:14,360
It means...

826
00:38:14,796 --> 00:38:17,498
anyone with cash can restock tokens
and start picking people off.

827
00:38:18,533 --> 00:38:21,235
Run out of funds,
and you're a sitting duck.

828
00:38:26,007 --> 00:38:26,940
And to make matters worse,

829
00:38:26,941 --> 00:38:30,277
by that point, it'll be almost
impossible to trace...

830
00:38:30,278 --> 00:38:32,646
who's firing shots within the system.

831
00:38:32,647 --> 00:38:34,282
- Exactly.
- Once we reach that stage.

832
00:38:37,485 --> 00:38:39,419
What are those two still whispering about?

833
00:38:40,855 --> 00:38:42,323
So that might be...

834
00:38:42,423 --> 00:38:44,490
- What are you two plotting over there?
- another angle.

835
00:38:44,491 --> 00:38:47,794
It's painfully obvious that circle...

836
00:38:48,396 --> 00:38:49,663
They carved out their own faction.

837
00:38:49,664 --> 00:38:51,531
- Nangja and Michael.
- Nangja, Michael,

838
00:38:51,532 --> 00:38:53,200
and Hippo are absolutely in lockstep.

839
00:38:53,201 --> 00:38:54,334
(Assuming Nangja, Michael, and Hippo
have formed an alliance.)

840
00:38:54,335 --> 00:38:56,670
We've got three votes in the bag,

841
00:38:56,671 --> 00:38:58,138
which gives us huge leverage.

842
00:38:58,139 --> 00:39:00,208
- We just need to peel off one more.
- That's completely within reach.

843
00:39:00,608 --> 00:39:02,043
And then you take
the Lifetime Leader seat.

844
00:39:02,176 --> 00:39:03,615
And then you take
the lifetime leader seat.

845
00:39:04,879 --> 00:39:08,715
How do you feel about going
head-to-head with Hippo?

846
00:39:09,484 --> 00:39:10,350
Why shouldn't I?

847
00:39:10,585 --> 00:39:11,952
Though honestly,

848
00:39:12,186 --> 00:39:14,455
once we pull this off and I get the title,

849
00:39:14,456 --> 00:39:16,256
I plan on taking a complete backseat.

850
00:39:16,257 --> 00:39:18,791
I'm not going to be
on the front lines picking fights.

851
00:39:18,792 --> 00:39:21,462
- More of an impartial mediator?
- That's the image I want to project.

852
00:39:22,163 --> 00:39:22,818
Sounds like a plan.

853
00:39:22,842 --> 00:39:24,765
We'll handle
the campaign ground game for you.

854
00:39:24,766 --> 00:39:26,709
You need at least four endorsements,

855
00:39:26,734 --> 00:39:27,802
excluding your own,

856
00:39:27,827 --> 00:39:30,036
- so we need one more person to flip.
- One more over.

857
00:39:30,037 --> 00:39:32,238
Since Genie's on the field team with us,

858
00:39:32,239 --> 00:39:33,540
- see if you can work on her.
- That crossed my mind too.

859
00:39:33,541 --> 00:39:35,041
Genie's a solid target.

860
00:39:35,042 --> 00:39:36,209
(Recruitment target: Genie)

861
00:39:36,210 --> 00:39:37,677
What about Sugar?

862
00:39:37,979 --> 00:39:39,255
Think we can butter her up?

863
00:39:39,280 --> 00:39:40,146
(Recruitment target: Sugar)

864
00:39:40,147 --> 00:39:41,115
Sounds like a plan.

865
00:39:41,215 --> 00:39:43,584
She keeps glancing over here.

866
00:39:43,683 --> 00:39:46,152
A second ago she went,
"I really hate this kind of tension."

867
00:39:47,021 --> 00:39:49,123
Give me a break.
She knows exactly what she's doing.

868
00:39:49,824 --> 00:39:51,592
She's easily the sharpest one in the room.

869
00:39:51,940 --> 00:39:53,026
Let's crush it.

870
00:39:53,027 --> 00:39:56,062
Who we get that final endorsement from
is everything.

871
00:39:56,864 --> 00:39:59,299
I genuinely think giving a platform
to contrarian voices...

872
00:39:59,300 --> 00:40:00,834
matters in a community like this.

873
00:40:00,835 --> 00:40:01,734
Thank you.

874
00:40:02,069 --> 00:40:03,203
(Michael's recruiter: Superman)

875
00:40:03,204 --> 00:40:03,970
You're with me.

876
00:40:04,172 --> 00:40:05,038
Yes, sir.

877
00:40:06,407 --> 00:40:08,309
Impeachment has to be on the table.

878
00:40:08,609 --> 00:40:11,711
I bet the spy's entire objective
is to spark an uprising...

879
00:40:11,712 --> 00:40:13,079
and topple the leader.

880
00:40:13,281 --> 00:40:15,282
First, I'm securing an immunity card.

881
00:40:15,283 --> 00:40:16,617
Who are you asking?

882
00:40:16,618 --> 00:40:17,484
Go Ae Shin.

883
00:40:17,685 --> 00:40:19,853
Good call. Definitely lock that down.

884
00:40:20,221 --> 00:40:21,488
This is all-out war now.

885
00:40:21,860 --> 00:40:23,061
We have to make sure we come out on top.

886
00:40:23,086 --> 00:40:24,357
You really need to sell her on it, though.

887
00:40:24,358 --> 00:40:27,260
If she thinks she's just
being used and discarded...

888
00:40:27,562 --> 00:40:29,063
You said you wanted to save someone.

889
00:40:29,064 --> 00:40:30,464
Can you use that card on me?

890
00:40:30,632 --> 00:40:31,220
Today?

891
00:40:31,244 --> 00:40:32,833
If I put my name down
as a candidate today,

892
00:40:32,834 --> 00:40:34,902
I'm painting a massive target
on my back for tomorrow.

893
00:40:35,303 --> 00:40:36,303
I'll use it on you, then.

894
00:40:36,304 --> 00:40:38,372
- If I run.
- Deal.

895
00:40:38,373 --> 00:40:41,675
(Superman secures his safety
with Go Ae Shin's immunity card.)

896
00:40:41,700 --> 00:40:42,600
Going great.

897
00:40:45,313 --> 00:40:47,014
I've been thinking about this.

898
00:40:47,015 --> 00:40:48,783
What if they bring in a military element?

899
00:40:49,116 --> 00:40:50,650
Dark Knight...

900
00:40:50,651 --> 00:40:53,519
He could oust the leader
and set up a military junta.

901
00:40:53,789 --> 00:40:54,722
A soldier taking charge.

902
00:40:54,723 --> 00:40:57,358
He probably already has...

903
00:40:57,359 --> 00:40:58,826
He definitely has people
in his pocket already.

904
00:40:58,827 --> 00:41:00,661
He's built his faction.

905
00:41:01,130 --> 00:41:04,298
The historical figure I respect most
is Benjamin Franklin.

906
00:41:05,133 --> 00:41:07,401
So I'm throwing my hat in the ring
for Lifetime Leader.

907
00:41:07,502 --> 00:41:08,802
If you back me as an endorser...

908
00:41:08,803 --> 00:41:13,607
I don't think this is a game
anyone can win flying solo.

909
00:41:14,042 --> 00:41:15,742
You need a broad coalition.

910
00:41:16,043 --> 00:41:18,177
I have a feeling they're forming
a military-backed bloc.

911
00:41:18,178 --> 00:41:20,446
I'm opening up to you because I know...

912
00:41:20,471 --> 00:41:22,849
you'd want to be
on the right side of history here.

913
00:41:23,077 --> 00:41:26,146
Right now, we suspect the spy is...

914
00:41:26,354 --> 00:41:27,854
either Hippo or Dark Knight.

915
00:41:28,456 --> 00:41:29,890
- So you need my vote?
- Exactly.

916
00:41:30,358 --> 00:41:32,092
Then keep me safe
if my neck is ever on the line.

917
00:41:32,093 --> 00:41:33,626
I always protect my people.
Don't worry about that.

918
00:41:33,651 --> 00:41:34,661
Understood.

919
00:41:34,662 --> 00:41:36,597
I hope we can align going forward.

920
00:41:36,964 --> 00:41:39,265
So what's the game plan?

921
00:41:39,266 --> 00:41:41,701
We fundamentally need
a ruling party and an opposition.

922
00:41:41,702 --> 00:41:43,069
That's not something
I can just spin up on my own.

923
00:41:43,070 --> 00:41:44,505
Leave that to me. I'll make it happen.

924
00:41:45,273 --> 00:41:46,841
You can take the helm
as the opposition leader.

925
00:41:46,992 --> 00:41:47,941
That makes sense.

926
00:41:47,942 --> 00:41:49,642
That could work really well.

927
00:41:49,643 --> 00:41:53,012
Plus, we can systematically curb
their faction's influence.

928
00:41:53,013 --> 00:41:54,781
You and I actually see eye-to-eye,

929
00:41:54,806 --> 00:41:56,060
so even if we play
opposing sides publicly,

930
00:41:56,085 --> 00:41:57,451
we can still cut deals
behind closed doors.

931
00:41:57,719 --> 00:42:00,887
You need to hit me hard
and challenge me openly.

932
00:42:00,888 --> 00:42:02,638
Meanwhile, I'll rally more people
to my side of the aisle.

933
00:42:02,663 --> 00:42:03,491
Precisely.

934
00:42:03,925 --> 00:42:05,292
That's how we control the entire board.

935
00:42:05,360 --> 00:42:07,060
I'll sound out Benjamin some more.

936
00:42:07,061 --> 00:42:08,162
Do it. Keep working on him.

937
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:13,967
Superman!

938
00:42:15,870 --> 00:42:16,837
It...

939
00:42:17,637 --> 00:42:18,639
has begun.

940
00:42:20,174 --> 00:42:22,008
- What do you mean?
- It's officially started.

941
00:42:22,777 --> 00:42:24,544
Game on.

942
00:42:24,979 --> 00:42:26,546
On Day Five, you declare...

943
00:42:30,518 --> 00:42:32,353
Wait, what if Benjamin is the spy?

944
00:42:35,356 --> 00:42:37,158
That would actually make a lot more sense.

945
00:42:37,658 --> 00:42:41,429
Benjamin, you've shown
what you're capable of...

946
00:42:42,029 --> 00:42:43,796
and built up immense goodwill,

947
00:42:43,965 --> 00:42:45,699
so nobody points fingers at you.

948
00:42:46,033 --> 00:42:47,668
You're always pulling strings
from the shadows.

949
00:42:48,803 --> 00:42:49,775
Could it be you?

950
00:42:51,005 --> 00:42:52,472
Did you two cut a deal?

951
00:42:54,542 --> 00:42:55,710
I've got my eye on you guys.

952
00:42:56,177 --> 00:42:59,012
If you prove your worth,
maybe I'll join your camp.

953
00:42:59,580 --> 00:43:02,782
I need to talk to Benjamin.

954
00:43:03,517 --> 00:43:05,284
Benjamin, we need to talk.

955
00:43:09,390 --> 00:43:10,825
- Can we step aside?
- Sure.

956
00:43:15,783 --> 00:43:17,351
Did those two just slip away together?

957
00:43:17,598 --> 00:43:18,531
Who?

958
00:43:19,500 --> 00:43:20,634
Who are you talking about?

959
00:43:20,735 --> 00:43:21,768
Nangja.

960
00:43:24,772 --> 00:43:27,441
Do you have questions for me,
or did you want to share something?

961
00:43:28,852 --> 00:43:31,387
We still don't know
what answers she got...

962
00:43:31,412 --> 00:43:32,679
to our questions.

963
00:43:32,680 --> 00:43:36,282
All our questions were tailored
to flush out the spy.

964
00:43:36,717 --> 00:43:38,894
And then she turns around
and slaps an embargo on it.

965
00:43:38,919 --> 00:43:40,453
"She must've found something."

966
00:43:40,788 --> 00:43:42,322
"She heard something massive."

967
00:43:42,424 --> 00:43:44,758
Like what? "This person is the spy."

968
00:43:44,925 --> 00:43:48,061
Nothing is set in stone yet,
so I need to dig deeper into...

969
00:43:48,062 --> 00:43:50,329
what's really driving her behavior.

970
00:43:53,100 --> 00:43:55,101
Why did you place an embargo?

971
00:43:55,336 --> 00:43:58,471
Calling an embargo was
a massive gamble for me.

972
00:43:58,472 --> 00:44:01,007
The trajectory completely changes
depending on whether I throw in...

973
00:44:01,008 --> 00:44:02,342
with the citizens or the spy.

974
00:44:02,710 --> 00:44:05,145
If the spy is in play...

975
00:44:05,146 --> 00:44:07,714
and knows what they're doing,

976
00:44:07,715 --> 00:44:10,384
they definitely picked up
on the signal I threw out.

977
00:44:12,787 --> 00:44:15,021
If our interests align,

978
00:44:15,322 --> 00:44:18,824
we can join forces
and flip the entire board.

979
00:44:19,927 --> 00:44:21,728
Even if she knows my identity,

980
00:44:21,729 --> 00:44:25,632
I couldn't validate or deny anything.

981
00:44:25,633 --> 00:44:27,600
I caught her drift immediately.

982
00:44:28,003 --> 00:44:30,136
I was left with only one move.

983
00:44:30,705 --> 00:44:35,042
I had no choice but to bring
the Reporter onto my side.

984
00:44:35,944 --> 00:44:37,978
Nangja must be trying to court Benjamin.

985
00:44:39,046 --> 00:44:43,249
She's trying to win over
the swing votes right now.

986
00:44:43,250 --> 00:44:44,518
Which means Nangja...

987
00:44:45,152 --> 00:44:48,055
is likely in bed with the spy.
We should operate on that assumption.

988
00:44:50,991 --> 00:44:52,658
Are we on the same page?

989
00:44:53,394 --> 00:44:54,928
I follow you now.

990
00:44:55,396 --> 00:44:57,697
You took a huge risk,

991
00:44:58,165 --> 00:45:02,269
so it's only fair that you get
something tangible in return.

992
00:45:02,270 --> 00:45:03,402
Agreed.

993
00:45:03,504 --> 00:45:05,572
How can I make this worth your while?

994
00:45:06,407 --> 00:45:07,942
I need an ironclad safety net...

995
00:45:08,142 --> 00:45:10,577
because I still have cards to play.

996
00:45:11,712 --> 00:45:14,747
I'll put together a deal you can't refuse.

997
00:45:14,982 --> 00:45:16,783
- Deal?
- Deal.

998
00:45:16,784 --> 00:45:21,020
I really need to think this through.

999
00:45:32,266 --> 00:45:33,566
This is...

1000
00:45:34,034 --> 00:45:35,067
tricky.

1001
00:45:35,770 --> 00:45:38,472
How do I make this leverage
feel reciprocal?

1002
00:45:42,977 --> 00:45:44,144
A parting message.

1003
00:45:46,180 --> 00:45:49,182
A dying message.

1004
00:45:50,251 --> 00:45:51,885
Let me test the waters with this first,

1005
00:45:52,553 --> 00:45:53,887
and if she steps out of line,

1006
00:45:54,288 --> 00:45:56,256
I take her out.

1007
00:45:57,558 --> 00:45:59,225
She probably sought me out
because she hit a dead end.

1008
00:45:59,226 --> 00:46:02,262
She was signaling, "I know who you are."

1009
00:46:02,263 --> 00:46:04,364
"I'm the one calling the shots here."

1010
00:46:04,365 --> 00:46:06,165
I think that was the message.

1011
00:46:06,267 --> 00:46:07,801
So I tabled an offer...

1012
00:46:08,102 --> 00:46:09,602
she couldn't turn down.

1013
00:46:09,603 --> 00:46:13,006
All that was left was convincing her
that we're stronger together.

1014
00:46:13,841 --> 00:46:14,842
I had the leverage.

1015
00:46:17,812 --> 00:46:20,447
The residents on the income team,

1016
00:46:20,614 --> 00:46:22,181
please head out now.

1017
00:46:22,182 --> 00:46:23,800
Whatever the challenge is,
I want to crush it.

1018
00:46:23,824 --> 00:46:24,517
Good luck out there!

1019
00:46:24,518 --> 00:46:25,552
- Let's roll!
- Let's do this.

1020
00:46:25,986 --> 00:46:26,987
You've got this.

1021
00:46:27,454 --> 00:46:33,626
(From the Community House
to the challenge site)

1022
00:46:34,328 --> 00:46:35,461
It's pure mountains out here.

1023
00:46:35,462 --> 00:46:37,864
We're literally in the middle of nowhere.

1024
00:46:37,865 --> 00:46:40,300
Just trekking up this hill has me on edge.

1025
00:46:40,301 --> 00:46:41,734
I'm winded already.

1026
00:46:41,735 --> 00:46:43,170
- That's not a great sign.
- Yes, ma'am.

1027
00:46:43,347 --> 00:46:44,103
Sorry!

1028
00:46:45,039 --> 00:46:47,040
Look, there's a structure up here.
An abandoned house?

1029
00:46:47,041 --> 00:46:48,875
Wait, is that our challenge site?

1030
00:46:49,076 --> 00:46:50,110
An abandoned building?

1031
00:46:50,511 --> 00:46:52,545
This feels like a haunted house special.

1032
00:46:52,746 --> 00:46:53,880
- Seriously.
- This place is...

1033
00:46:53,881 --> 00:46:56,115
- straight-up derelict.
- Superman would dominate here.

1034
00:46:56,116 --> 00:46:57,116
- Totally.
- One-hundred percent.

1035
00:46:58,084 --> 00:47:00,553
It looks so eerie.

1036
00:47:01,555 --> 00:47:03,457
We really should've brought
Dark Knight along.

1037
00:47:03,724 --> 00:47:05,759
It's for 100,000 dollars.
They're not handing it to us.

1038
00:47:05,760 --> 00:47:08,228
This is way too creepy, though.

1039
00:47:09,763 --> 00:47:11,199
This spot is terrifying.

1040
00:47:11,200 --> 00:47:12,732
Way spookier than the building down below.

1041
00:47:12,733 --> 00:47:15,067
- This is wild.
- There's the entrance.

1042
00:47:15,068 --> 00:47:16,535
- Found it.
- We're going in there?

1043
00:47:16,837 --> 00:47:19,639
I'm terrified and hyped at the same time.

1044
00:47:23,912 --> 00:47:25,379
This is wild.

1045
00:47:25,380 --> 00:47:27,614
Wait, what is all this?

1046
00:47:27,615 --> 00:47:28,748
Holy...

1047
00:47:29,683 --> 00:47:31,285
- What's in there?
- What is that?

1048
00:47:31,518 --> 00:47:32,685
The Korean flag?

1049
00:47:32,686 --> 00:47:35,120
I called it. There's a world map.
The US, Japan.

1050
00:47:35,121 --> 00:47:37,456
"Today's Income-Earning Activity:
Trek Across the Globe."

1051
00:47:38,034 --> 00:47:39,803
- What do we have here?
- What's this?

1052
00:47:39,828 --> 00:47:40,793
(Map packs and walkie-talkies)

1053
00:47:40,794 --> 00:47:42,830
Dark Knight really
should've been on this squad.

1054
00:47:43,231 --> 00:47:46,499
Today's income-earning activity is
"Trek Across the Globe."

1055
00:47:46,500 --> 00:47:47,567
You have a two-hour time limit.

1056
00:47:47,568 --> 00:47:48,968
Trek Across the Globe is...

1057
00:47:49,370 --> 00:47:51,871
a mission testing your grasp
of global statistics...

1058
00:47:51,872 --> 00:47:53,239
from around the world.

1059
00:47:54,541 --> 00:47:59,688
Rule one, five countries are
marked on the map,

1060
00:47:59,713 --> 00:48:00,813
including Korea.

1061
00:48:01,148 --> 00:48:04,917
In each country,
there are five color-coded lockboxes.

1062
00:48:05,218 --> 00:48:09,021
Each lockbox corresponds
to a specific national statistic.

1063
00:48:09,456 --> 00:48:12,558
Rule two, once the clock starts,

1064
00:48:12,826 --> 00:48:14,260
you will select...

1065
00:48:14,528 --> 00:48:17,630
the keys you believe match
each country's statistics...

1066
00:48:17,933 --> 00:48:19,867
and travel to that country's station
with them.

1067
00:48:20,367 --> 00:48:22,601
Find the right key,
and you unlock the box...

1068
00:48:22,602 --> 00:48:24,070
to claim the symbol inside.

1069
00:48:24,805 --> 00:48:26,906
Each player may carry...

1070
00:48:27,041 --> 00:48:28,775
a maximum of five keys at a time.

1071
00:48:29,476 --> 00:48:32,611
Rule three, once you secure the symbols,

1072
00:48:32,612 --> 00:48:36,182
bring them back to Korea
and mount them on the board.

1073
00:48:37,351 --> 00:48:40,486
Completing all five symbols
for a single country...

1074
00:48:40,955 --> 00:48:44,591
awards 20,000 dollars for that country.

1075
00:48:45,225 --> 00:48:47,827
Rule four, because Korea serves
as your home base,

1076
00:48:47,828 --> 00:48:50,062
its boxes cannot be opened first.

1077
00:48:50,431 --> 00:48:53,166
Determine which keys are duds...

1078
00:48:53,167 --> 00:48:57,570
and retain only the ones
you believe match Korea's statistics.

1079
00:48:58,839 --> 00:49:01,941
- Got it.
- Break it down for me.

1080
00:49:01,942 --> 00:49:04,310
When we hit a country's station,
we'll see sets of locks just like these.

1081
00:49:04,335 --> 00:49:08,038
Our goal is cracking open
the lockboxes in each country.

1082
00:49:08,482 --> 00:49:11,384
Use the key you think represents
the correct answer...

1083
00:49:11,385 --> 00:49:13,586
to that color's question.

1084
00:49:13,587 --> 00:49:15,288
If the key won't turn,
that answer is incorrect.

1085
00:49:15,289 --> 00:49:17,690
What is the annual per capita
pork consumption in the US?

1086
00:49:18,092 --> 00:49:20,060
What is the gender wage gap in the US?

1087
00:49:20,061 --> 00:49:22,462
Every correct answer unlocks one box.

1088
00:49:22,463 --> 00:49:25,865
Then we haul the symbols back here
to mount on the master board.

1089
00:49:25,866 --> 00:49:27,600
- Exactly.
- We should complete columns vertically...

1090
00:49:27,601 --> 00:49:28,824
instead of filling rows horizontally,

1091
00:49:28,849 --> 00:49:30,386
because each completed country
pays out 20,000 dollars.

1092
00:49:30,411 --> 00:49:32,338
- One country at a time.
- We need every single one right.

1093
00:49:32,339 --> 00:49:33,606
Mapping an efficient route.

1094
00:49:33,607 --> 00:49:35,575
Passing keys off to each other on the run.

1095
00:49:35,576 --> 00:49:37,111
Stamina combined with trivia knowledge.

1096
00:49:37,112 --> 00:49:39,479
If we coordinate all of that,
we can sweep this.

1097
00:49:39,913 --> 00:49:41,113
And now,

1098
00:49:41,749 --> 00:49:43,717
your two-hour countdown begins.

1099
00:49:43,718 --> 00:49:44,584
Starting now?

1100
00:49:45,119 --> 00:49:47,020
Safe travels.

1101
00:49:50,491 --> 00:49:53,093
Should we pool what we know
to start eliminating wrong answers?

1102
00:49:53,094 --> 00:49:54,093
Sounds like a plan.

1103
00:49:54,094 --> 00:49:56,162
- Starting with the nearest options...
- Israel.

1104
00:49:56,163 --> 00:49:57,964
"Average per capita pork consumption."
It definitely isn't zero.

1105
00:49:57,965 --> 00:49:59,768
- It's a Middle Eastern nation.
- But not a Muslim one.

1106
00:49:59,793 --> 00:50:00,866
No Muslim-majority countries on the list.

1107
00:50:00,867 --> 00:50:02,469
Are these numbers distances from here?

1108
00:50:02,470 --> 00:50:03,603
Isn't Japan the closest geographically?

1109
00:50:03,604 --> 00:50:04,804
Let's tackle Japan first, then.

1110
00:50:04,805 --> 00:50:06,339
When it comes to the gender wage gap,

1111
00:50:06,340 --> 00:50:08,308
Korea ranks near the bottom.

1112
00:50:08,309 --> 00:50:09,843
Japan is probably sitting in fourth.

1113
00:50:09,844 --> 00:50:12,412
Their wage disparity is marginally
better than Korea's,

1114
00:50:12,413 --> 00:50:13,614
but still in the same ballpark.

1115
00:50:13,647 --> 00:50:15,681
Where would Mexico land?

1116
00:50:15,682 --> 00:50:16,816
The gap might actually be narrower there.

1117
00:50:16,817 --> 00:50:18,952
Their overall wage baseline is lower,

1118
00:50:18,953 --> 00:50:20,386
so the statistical spread isn't as wide.

1119
00:50:20,387 --> 00:50:22,055
The gap is relative,

1120
00:50:22,080 --> 00:50:23,535
so if everyone earns very little
across the board,

1121
00:50:23,560 --> 00:50:24,423
the disparity stays small.

1122
00:50:24,424 --> 00:50:25,391
Their life expectancy has to be high.

1123
00:50:25,392 --> 00:50:27,059
- It's bound to be high.
- Japan's life expectancy...

1124
00:50:27,060 --> 00:50:28,861
- Must be high.
- It's got to be the highest.

1125
00:50:28,862 --> 00:50:30,397
- Then it has to be 92.
- The highest on the board is 92.4.

1126
00:50:30,597 --> 00:50:32,531
Wait. What are the other options?

1127
00:50:32,532 --> 00:50:34,934
There's 84.5 and 82.6.

1128
00:50:34,935 --> 00:50:36,068
Most of these cluster in the 80s.

1129
00:50:36,069 --> 00:50:38,104
I really don't think
it breaks into the 90s.

1130
00:50:38,105 --> 00:50:40,773
Let's assume it sits in the 80s.

1131
00:50:40,774 --> 00:50:42,042
All three of these options are in the 80s.

1132
00:50:42,043 --> 00:50:44,143
Then let's lock this in
for Japan's life expectancy.

1133
00:50:44,144 --> 00:50:45,745
We don't have as much time as we think.
Let's tackle one country...

1134
00:50:45,746 --> 00:50:47,523
- and see how long it takes.
- We've got two full hours.

1135
00:50:47,548 --> 00:50:49,482
Want to hear why I don't think that works?

1136
00:50:49,483 --> 00:50:52,051
We only have two hours,
and we're guessing in the dark.

1137
00:50:52,052 --> 00:50:53,686
Each runner can only carry
five keys at a time.

1138
00:50:53,821 --> 00:50:56,822
So let's leave one person behind,
have one person run relay...

1139
00:50:56,823 --> 00:50:59,476
between base and the field crew,

1140
00:50:59,501 --> 00:51:00,993
and send the other three
straight to the stations.

1141
00:51:00,994 --> 00:51:03,329
The relay runner holds
the master key set...

1142
00:51:03,330 --> 00:51:05,764
and sprints over with spares
if a key fails.

1143
00:51:05,765 --> 00:51:07,601
Then I'll take
per capita pork consumption.

1144
00:51:08,101 --> 00:51:09,202
Let's run a test run...

1145
00:51:09,203 --> 00:51:11,170
- since 900 meters is an easy sprint.
- Sounds like a plan.

1146
00:51:11,171 --> 00:51:13,039
Genie should hold down headquarters...

1147
00:51:13,040 --> 00:51:14,874
and manage logistics.

1148
00:51:14,875 --> 00:51:17,010
The rest of us sprint.

1149
00:51:17,011 --> 00:51:19,912
There's a map in the pack.
Keep eyes on it.

1150
00:51:19,913 --> 00:51:22,248
I'll take the keys we slotted for Japan.

1151
00:51:22,249 --> 00:51:24,083
We need to get the lock mechanics down.

1152
00:51:24,418 --> 00:51:25,385
Got it!

1153
00:51:25,619 --> 00:51:27,587
Can we actually pull this off
within two hours?

1154
00:51:27,588 --> 00:51:29,121
Someone needs to hit Japan.

1155
00:51:29,122 --> 00:51:30,890
- I'll run with you.
- Let's go.

1156
00:51:31,191 --> 00:51:33,025
This map routes along the paved roads.

1157
00:51:33,026 --> 00:51:35,461
(The team sets out toward Japan.)

1158
00:51:36,530 --> 00:51:38,464
Man, this is brutal.

1159
00:51:42,102 --> 00:51:43,846
They're just idling back at base
right now.

1160
00:51:43,871 --> 00:51:45,438
There's no point in them
just sitting there.

1161
00:51:45,439 --> 00:51:47,373
- Israel is up that direction.
- Right.

1162
00:51:47,374 --> 00:51:48,842
Why not send them straight there?

1163
00:51:48,843 --> 00:51:50,844
Understood. I'll follow your call.

1164
00:51:52,312 --> 00:51:53,946
Headquarters, reading me?

1165
00:51:54,181 --> 00:51:55,248
Loud and clear.

1166
00:51:55,915 --> 00:51:58,953
Superman grabbed the candidate keys
and took off after you.

1167
00:51:58,978 --> 00:52:00,120
Keep that in mind.

1168
00:52:00,787 --> 00:52:01,655
Copy that.

1169
00:52:03,257 --> 00:52:07,193
(Convenience store)

1170
00:52:07,194 --> 00:52:09,963
Don't hit the convenience store.
Take the side path to the left.

1171
00:52:10,664 --> 00:52:13,199
We're en route.
Once you spot the 7-Eleven sign,

1172
00:52:13,566 --> 00:52:15,535
make a left.

1173
00:52:17,504 --> 00:52:18,572
Appreciate it.

1174
00:52:20,174 --> 00:52:21,069
Man.

1175
00:52:33,086 --> 00:52:34,488
I'm running on fumes already.

1176
00:52:34,988 --> 00:52:36,889
The address is Incho-ri 233-4.

1177
00:52:40,661 --> 00:52:42,195
This looks like the fork.

1178
00:52:45,365 --> 00:52:46,265
Excuse me!

1179
00:52:46,700 --> 00:52:49,102
Ma'am, the convenience store
is this way, right?

1180
00:52:49,903 --> 00:52:52,671
Excuse me, do you know
how to get here from this street?

1181
00:52:53,240 --> 00:52:54,908
(Using every resource available)

1182
00:52:54,909 --> 00:52:56,075
Japan is down this way.

1183
00:52:56,076 --> 00:52:57,777
I've got the keys.

1184
00:52:57,945 --> 00:53:00,079
We're right here.
Here's the split toward Japan.

1185
00:53:00,080 --> 00:53:01,414
- I brought the whole key set.
- Let's move!

1186
00:53:05,252 --> 00:53:06,386
It's right around here.

1187
00:53:06,553 --> 00:53:07,587
Spotted it!

1188
00:53:08,288 --> 00:53:09,255
- Japan is here!
- Found Japan!

1189
00:53:09,256 --> 00:53:10,556
We're at Japan.

1190
00:53:11,458 --> 00:53:12,558
You found Japan already?

1191
00:53:12,559 --> 00:53:14,326
- Yes, we're on site.
- We made it.

1192
00:53:14,862 --> 00:53:15,761
They've reached the Japan station.

1193
00:53:15,762 --> 00:53:16,629
(The key crew arrives at Japan.)

1194
00:53:17,631 --> 00:53:18,586
We're at Japan.

1195
00:53:18,610 --> 00:53:20,433
One hour and thirty-two minutes
on the clock.

1196
00:53:21,101 --> 00:53:23,769
It took you guys roughly nine minutes...

1197
00:53:23,770 --> 00:53:25,604
to cover 900 meters.

1198
00:53:25,605 --> 00:53:26,839
Nine minutes for 900 meters?

1199
00:53:26,840 --> 00:53:29,041
(Nine minutes spent reaching Japan)

1200
00:53:29,476 --> 00:53:31,377
Japan's life expectancy is 84.5.

1201
00:53:31,378 --> 00:53:32,511
(Life expectancy: Japan, 84.5 years)

1202
00:53:32,512 --> 00:53:35,281
Log that down, Japan, 84.5.

1203
00:53:37,517 --> 00:53:40,385
Annual per capita alcohol consumption,
10.09 liters.

1204
00:53:40,386 --> 00:53:42,421
(Annual per capita alcohol consumption:
Japan, 10.09 liters)

1205
00:53:43,090 --> 00:53:45,958
Per capita pork consumption,
Japan, 15.76 kilograms.

1206
00:53:46,660 --> 00:53:48,394
Gender wage gap. Japan ranks fourth.

1207
00:53:48,695 --> 00:53:50,663
Annual working hours,
Japan logs 1,607 hours.

1208
00:53:50,664 --> 00:53:51,730
(Average annual hours worked per worker:
Japan, 1,607 hours)

1209
00:53:51,755 --> 00:53:54,557
(All five lockboxes opened
at the Japan station.)

1210
00:53:54,936 --> 00:53:58,070
I'm still trying to piece this together.

1211
00:53:58,071 --> 00:54:00,439
Does the spy crack open a code
every single day?

1212
00:54:00,440 --> 00:54:03,009
The notice said they accessed a code today
just like yesterday.

1213
00:54:03,010 --> 00:54:04,076
They might get one every day.

1214
00:54:04,077 --> 00:54:06,446
Doesn't that feel completely
tilted in their favor?

1215
00:54:06,747 --> 00:54:09,315
The biggest takeaway from this...

1216
00:54:09,316 --> 00:54:11,551
is that we need an immunity card
locked down in advance.

1217
00:54:11,984 --> 00:54:13,185
An immunity card?

1218
00:54:13,186 --> 00:54:14,620
Yeah, secure an immunity card.

1219
00:54:14,621 --> 00:54:18,290
The problem is, today we don't have
solid leverage to ask for one.

1220
00:54:18,291 --> 00:54:21,894
I can't just beg for it
out of pure paranoia.

1221
00:54:21,895 --> 00:54:24,197
Michael, I can hand mine over to you
if you're in the crosshairs.

1222
00:54:24,398 --> 00:54:25,298
Are you serious?

1223
00:54:25,632 --> 00:54:27,634
Just let me know
if you truly feel targeted.

1224
00:54:28,568 --> 00:54:30,669
Nobody in this house...

1225
00:54:30,670 --> 00:54:33,238
is as steady and genuine as she is.

1226
00:54:33,408 --> 00:54:35,374
On night three,
she told me straight out...

1227
00:54:35,375 --> 00:54:37,913
that she'd step in...

1228
00:54:37,914 --> 00:54:41,681
and use her immunity card on me
whenever I needed it.

1229
00:54:41,682 --> 00:54:44,150
You stood your ground and spoke up,

1230
00:54:44,151 --> 00:54:46,620
even knowing full well
the target it put on your back.

1231
00:54:46,621 --> 00:54:48,688
I think that took serious guts.

1232
00:54:48,855 --> 00:54:51,557
If your neck is ever on the line,
I'm burning my card for you.

1233
00:54:51,825 --> 00:54:53,059
Don't worry about it, okay?

1234
00:54:53,627 --> 00:54:56,295
Polar Bear, I can't thank you enough.

1235
00:54:56,463 --> 00:54:58,698
Stop, really. I barely did anything.

1236
00:54:58,799 --> 00:55:00,900
Anyone can talk a big game like that,

1237
00:55:00,901 --> 00:55:03,970
but very few people actually mean it.

1238
00:55:03,971 --> 00:55:04,804
It's genuinely inspiring.

1239
00:55:04,805 --> 00:55:07,073
Polar Bear has a massive heart.

1240
00:55:07,475 --> 00:55:08,708
She doesn't play dirty.

1241
00:55:09,109 --> 00:55:11,810
Like you already know,

1242
00:55:11,811 --> 00:55:15,013
I'm not into forming rigid alliances...

1243
00:55:15,014 --> 00:55:17,249
or being boxed into someone's faction.

1244
00:55:17,250 --> 00:55:20,319
I'm always open to teaming up with anyone.

1245
00:55:20,320 --> 00:55:21,253
I think...

1246
00:55:21,254 --> 00:55:21,876
Agreed.

1247
00:55:21,900 --> 00:55:23,922
we can build real mutual trust
on that foundation.

1248
00:55:24,658 --> 00:55:26,626
- I'll keep my word.
- Right back at you.

1249
00:55:31,331 --> 00:55:33,399
Do you have the keys...

1250
00:55:33,400 --> 00:55:35,501
for the US on you?

1251
00:55:35,902 --> 00:55:38,770
I've got 82.6 and 83.6
for life expectancy.

1252
00:55:38,771 --> 00:55:40,405
I have second place
for the gender wage gap.

1253
00:55:40,607 --> 00:55:41,606
Can we pivot to the US?

1254
00:55:41,607 --> 00:55:42,741
Let's round up the keys
and push toward the US.

1255
00:55:42,742 --> 00:55:44,510
Part of the crew runs to the US.
The rest haul back.

1256
00:55:44,511 --> 00:55:46,246
- Let's sprint to the US station.
- Straight to the US?

1257
00:55:46,346 --> 00:55:48,013
We're moving to the US.

1258
00:55:48,014 --> 00:55:49,815
(En route to the US station)

1259
00:55:50,049 --> 00:55:53,251
The remaining options
for the gender wage gap...

1260
00:55:53,252 --> 00:55:55,854
are 1st, 7th, and 12th.

1261
00:55:55,855 --> 00:55:57,556
Which one belongs to the US?

1262
00:55:57,557 --> 00:55:59,958
For the US gender wage gap:
1st, 7th, or 12th.

1263
00:55:59,959 --> 00:56:02,629
The runner stationed at base
needs to bring those keys down.

1264
00:56:02,630 --> 00:56:03,495
Genie.

1265
00:56:03,496 --> 00:56:05,664
I'm currently heading back to base.

1266
00:56:05,665 --> 00:56:07,399
Why don't you head down right now...

1267
00:56:07,400 --> 00:56:09,167
with the keys you think fit the US?

1268
00:56:09,168 --> 00:56:11,470
Copy that. I'll head down
with the keys from here.

1269
00:56:16,343 --> 00:56:18,577
(Convenience store)

1270
00:56:18,578 --> 00:56:20,132
(The pavilion,
the village community center)

1271
00:56:20,132 --> 00:56:21,812
(The bridge, the tofu diner)

1272
00:56:23,016 --> 00:56:24,317
I think it's just past there.

1273
00:56:24,584 --> 00:56:25,852
We're on the right trail, yeah?

1274
00:56:26,553 --> 00:56:27,954
Follow along the right side of the stream.

1275
00:56:28,288 --> 00:56:29,088
The US station!

1276
00:56:29,323 --> 00:56:30,756
- US is right here!
- Found the US!

1277
00:56:31,958 --> 00:56:35,527
(The field crew reaches the US station)

1278
00:56:36,196 --> 00:56:37,029
Unlocked.

1279
00:56:37,397 --> 00:56:39,064
US alcohol consumption, 9.97 liters.

1280
00:56:39,599 --> 00:56:40,799
- Nice.
- Got it.

1281
00:56:41,134 --> 00:56:41,967
Next one.

1282
00:56:42,369 --> 00:56:43,469
Life expectancy unlocked!

1283
00:56:43,636 --> 00:56:45,771
(Life expectancy:
United States, 76.4 years)

1284
00:56:45,772 --> 00:56:47,072
(Average annual per capita
pork consumption)

1285
00:56:47,073 --> 00:56:48,206
Pork consumption.

1286
00:56:48,909 --> 00:56:49,675
US box.

1287
00:56:50,010 --> 00:56:50,843
It's open!

1288
00:56:51,178 --> 00:56:52,879
- What's the stat?
- It's 1,811 hours.

1289
00:56:52,880 --> 00:56:54,381
Let's pile them up over there.

1290
00:56:55,182 --> 00:56:57,016
The US isn't eighth.

1291
00:56:57,484 --> 00:56:59,318
Wait, on this one? The gender wage gap?

1292
00:57:02,622 --> 00:57:04,990
The US doesn't rank eighth
on the gender wage gap.

1293
00:57:04,991 --> 00:57:07,125
How many keys for the US are on you?

1294
00:57:07,327 --> 00:57:08,861
I haven't reached headquarters yet.

1295
00:57:08,862 --> 00:57:10,796
I'm about five minutes out.

1296
00:57:10,797 --> 00:57:12,531
Genie is holding them.

1297
00:57:13,200 --> 00:57:15,535
It's between 1st, 7th, and 12th.

1298
00:57:17,037 --> 00:57:19,005
- Keep pushing straight.
- Straight ahead?

1299
00:57:19,539 --> 00:57:20,606
- Toward the US?
- Yes, go!

1300
00:57:20,607 --> 00:57:22,141
(Hippo and Genie intercept each other.)

1301
00:57:22,976 --> 00:57:24,243
Genie is en route.

1302
00:57:25,813 --> 00:57:27,179
It was 100,000 dollars on the line.

1303
00:57:27,180 --> 00:57:31,183
We had to take that prize pool home,
no matter what it took.

1304
00:57:31,184 --> 00:57:32,918
I was completely locked in to win.

1305
00:57:32,919 --> 00:57:34,909
There was no way
I was going to half-ass it...

1306
00:57:34,933 --> 00:57:36,455
and watch that kind of money slip away.

1307
00:57:36,456 --> 00:57:39,124
I can't call it a mistake
or plead ignorance.

1308
00:57:39,125 --> 00:57:40,826
It would all come down to...

1309
00:57:40,827 --> 00:57:42,328
whether I gave it everything I had.

1310
00:57:42,329 --> 00:57:44,130
I had no excuses to hide behind.

1311
00:57:44,131 --> 00:57:46,265
That payout was what drove everyone
to push so hard.

1312
00:57:46,566 --> 00:57:49,034
We all knew our performance...

1313
00:57:49,035 --> 00:57:52,337
in these challenges would be judged
by the house crew.

1314
00:57:52,338 --> 00:57:54,406
It was about proving our worth,

1315
00:57:54,407 --> 00:57:56,308
our presence, and our commitment
to the team.

1316
00:57:56,309 --> 00:57:57,309
(Nangja: Right, Egalitarian,
Working Class, Conservative)

1317
00:57:57,978 --> 00:58:00,813
<i>I went</i>

1318
00:58:01,815 --> 00:58:03,749
<i>Through the roof</i>

1319
00:58:18,798 --> 00:58:20,866
(Benjamin walks in.)

1320
00:58:20,867 --> 00:58:21,800
Hey there.

1321
00:58:22,235 --> 00:58:24,770
- What are you up to?
- Not much.

1322
00:58:25,805 --> 00:58:27,639
- Did you hear?
- Hear what?

1323
00:58:28,325 --> 00:58:30,243
We're going authoritarian now.

1324
00:58:30,912 --> 00:58:33,212
Should we step into the chat room?

1325
00:58:33,446 --> 00:58:35,180
The one at the far end.

1326
00:58:35,816 --> 00:58:37,817
Isn't Polar Bear's room
right across from yours?

1327
00:58:37,818 --> 00:58:40,152
- Yeah, it is.
- Could you knock on her door?

1328
00:58:40,153 --> 00:58:41,720
Sure, let me check.

1329
00:58:41,721 --> 00:58:44,390
(Benjamin)

1330
00:58:44,391 --> 00:58:47,994
(Polar Bear)

1331
00:58:47,995 --> 00:58:48,961
Who is it?

1332
00:58:53,934 --> 00:58:55,334
Polar Bear, so sorry to bother you.

1333
00:58:55,835 --> 00:58:59,137
Gray wants to talk to us.

1334
00:58:59,138 --> 00:59:01,006
- Sure.
- Were you sleeping?

1335
00:59:05,178 --> 00:59:07,412
This room is more private.

1336
00:59:08,848 --> 00:59:11,316
She was asleep. I feel awful.

1337
00:59:13,019 --> 00:59:15,754
Polar Bear will fill you in,

1338
00:59:15,755 --> 00:59:19,491
but Superman said he wants
to throw his hat in the ring.

1339
00:59:19,492 --> 00:59:20,693
We need four endorsements...

1340
00:59:20,694 --> 00:59:23,695
to officially put our person
on the ballot.

1341
00:59:23,696 --> 00:59:24,730
(Candidates require four endorsements
to qualify)

1342
00:59:24,731 --> 00:59:26,865
He said if he runs...

1343
00:59:28,602 --> 00:59:29,802
I'm so sorry.

1344
00:59:29,803 --> 00:59:31,070
Don't worry about it.

1345
00:59:31,071 --> 00:59:31,904
You're fine.

1346
00:59:32,306 --> 00:59:35,240
I'm sorry, but I figured this was
our only window to talk.

1347
00:59:35,241 --> 00:59:36,975
I get it. It's totally fine.

1348
00:59:36,976 --> 00:59:39,178
Here's how I see it.

1349
00:59:39,613 --> 00:59:41,881
If there are two candidates...

1350
00:59:41,882 --> 00:59:43,716
If we lock our person in,

1351
00:59:43,717 --> 00:59:46,018
and the other side backs Ted
or someone else...

1352
00:59:46,820 --> 00:59:48,320
to get on the ballot...

1353
00:59:48,321 --> 00:59:50,055
It's not the actual vote yet anyway.

1354
00:59:50,056 --> 00:59:53,526
Right now, it's just
about qualifying someone to run.

1355
00:59:53,527 --> 00:59:57,029
As long as four people endorse them,
they make the cut.

1356
00:59:57,330 --> 00:59:59,998
You need at least seven votes
to win the election.

1357
00:59:59,999 --> 01:00:01,667
For now, we're just picking...

1358
01:00:02,335 --> 01:00:04,202
who we want to put forward.

1359
01:00:04,203 --> 01:00:07,072
I'm a little lost. So all I need to do
is endorse Superman?

1360
01:00:07,073 --> 01:00:09,508
- That's the plan.
- Is that settled?

1361
01:00:09,509 --> 01:00:10,676
Yeah, we're locked in.

1362
01:00:10,677 --> 01:00:13,178
What I want to know is...

1363
01:00:13,179 --> 01:00:15,380
I don't mind backing Superman,
as you said.

1364
01:00:15,381 --> 01:00:17,816
What's your case for supporting him?

1365
01:00:18,184 --> 01:00:19,785
Our reason for backing him isn't really...

1366
01:00:19,786 --> 01:00:21,253
about vetting his abilities.

1367
01:00:21,254 --> 01:00:22,621
It comes down to trust...

1368
01:00:22,622 --> 01:00:24,756
- backing someone from our own camp.
- We need someone in our corner.

1369
01:00:24,757 --> 01:00:26,391
If I'm picking someone,

1370
01:00:26,994 --> 01:00:29,762
I want someone who has my back.

1371
01:00:29,763 --> 01:00:30,663
Exactly.

1372
01:00:30,664 --> 01:00:34,166
The people on board with this...

1373
01:00:34,167 --> 01:00:38,604
are the ones who, up until yesterday,
fought to keep everyone safe.

1374
01:00:38,605 --> 01:00:40,905
That's the trust we're leaning on...

1375
01:00:40,906 --> 01:00:44,276
to push this candidate forward.

1376
01:00:44,277 --> 01:00:48,213
We need a firm commitment
from our candidate.

1377
01:00:48,314 --> 01:00:50,582
He'll definitely agree to that.

1378
01:00:50,583 --> 01:00:53,251
That should be an easy sell.

1379
01:00:53,252 --> 01:00:55,320
Looking around at who...

1380
01:00:55,321 --> 01:00:58,823
we actually feel safe with,

1381
01:00:58,824 --> 01:01:00,525
it's you, Gray,

1382
01:01:01,027 --> 01:01:03,161
Ted, Genie,

1383
01:01:03,330 --> 01:01:05,731
Go Ae Shin, Superman, and me.

1384
01:01:06,499 --> 01:01:07,899
That makes...

1385
01:01:09,002 --> 01:01:10,002
seven of us.

1386
01:01:10,003 --> 01:01:11,403
(The Superman coalition)

1387
01:01:11,404 --> 01:01:12,705
(Benjamin, Gray, Ted, Genie,
Go Ae Shin, Superman, Polar Bear)

1388
01:01:12,939 --> 01:01:14,640
Then I'll consider this...

1389
01:01:14,641 --> 01:01:18,076
the core coalition backing him.

1390
01:01:18,244 --> 01:01:19,844
Sounds like a plan.

1391
01:01:19,845 --> 01:01:21,079
The numbers are already there.

1392
01:01:21,714 --> 01:01:23,550
I don't think...

1393
01:01:23,551 --> 01:01:26,852
I can go around convincing
everyone myself.

1394
01:01:26,853 --> 01:01:28,287
Don't worry. We'll handle the legwork.

1395
01:01:28,288 --> 01:01:29,988
I'm on board with your plan.

1396
01:01:30,023 --> 01:01:32,358
- I'll back him.
- Thanks, Benjamin.

1397
01:01:33,093 --> 01:01:33,893
Sounds good.

1398
01:01:34,127 --> 01:01:36,128
- Sorry again for waking you up.
- Don't sweat it.

1399
01:01:36,724 --> 01:01:37,697
I'm wide awake now anyway.

1400
01:01:37,698 --> 01:01:38,998
Go try to get some more sleep.

1401
01:01:40,333 --> 01:01:42,768
Hey! Great work out there!

1402
01:01:43,003 --> 01:01:45,037
- Good job, guys.
- Thanks.

1403
01:01:45,038 --> 01:01:47,272
How much did you bring back?
If it's peanuts, you're in trouble.

1404
01:01:47,941 --> 01:01:49,708
Please, spare us!

1405
01:01:50,577 --> 01:01:52,044
Look, I just want to apologize first.

1406
01:01:52,545 --> 01:01:54,112
Come on, why are you starting
with an apology?

1407
01:01:54,280 --> 01:01:56,081
- Was it physically grueling?
- Pure cardio.

1408
01:01:56,082 --> 01:01:57,582
Brutal physical labor.

1409
01:01:57,583 --> 01:02:00,685
- And Dark Knight sat this one out.
- Tell me about it.

1410
01:02:00,686 --> 01:02:02,888
He's built for raw physical challenges.

1411
01:02:02,889 --> 01:02:05,222
- Sounds like you should've gone.
- I know.

1412
01:02:05,223 --> 01:02:07,259
What can you do? We all live and learn.

1413
01:02:07,260 --> 01:02:08,293
I made...

1414
01:02:08,294 --> 01:02:10,629
- a huge blunder right at the end.
- Superman, you did great.

1415
01:02:10,630 --> 01:02:12,264
- Seriously, man.
- It's all good.

1416
01:02:12,265 --> 01:02:14,766
We really put the ladies
through the wringer.

1417
01:02:16,269 --> 01:02:18,503
I'm drenched in sweat.

1418
01:02:18,504 --> 01:02:20,439
After putting in that kind of work,

1419
01:02:21,241 --> 01:02:23,209
we really should've walked away with more.

1420
01:02:23,210 --> 01:02:27,112
We couldn't believe how little we made.

1421
01:02:27,113 --> 01:02:29,682
Wait, wasn't the prize
pot 100,000 dollars?

1422
01:02:29,683 --> 01:02:32,585
Anyone out there would've struggled.

1423
01:02:32,586 --> 01:02:34,688
Do you just want us to break the bad news?

1424
01:02:34,689 --> 01:02:37,089
Honestly,
ripping off the band-aid is easier.

1425
01:02:38,858 --> 01:02:40,458
We're really sorry.

1426
01:02:40,459 --> 01:02:42,594
- We messed up.
- Forgive us.

1427
01:02:42,929 --> 01:02:45,631
Today's total earnings come out...

1428
01:02:45,632 --> 01:02:46,498
Good effort, guys.

1429
01:02:49,169 --> 01:02:51,036
to 80,000 dollars!

1430
01:02:51,037 --> 01:02:53,572
(Total prize money: 80,000 dollars)

1431
01:02:56,776 --> 01:02:57,976
You had us going!

1432
01:03:00,747 --> 01:03:01,980
I mean, 80,000 dollars?

1433
01:03:02,382 --> 01:03:03,449
Wait, what?

1434
01:03:03,450 --> 01:03:05,884
- We brought home 80,000 dollars!
- Let's go!

1435
01:03:05,885 --> 01:03:07,019
(Largest payout to date)

1436
01:03:07,020 --> 01:03:08,821
We're feasting tonight!

1437
01:03:09,956 --> 01:03:12,057
(The house erupts in celebration)

1438
01:03:12,058 --> 01:03:14,626
- They crushed it.
- Yay, 80,000 dollars!

1439
01:03:14,627 --> 01:03:16,762
I completely fell for it.
Got actual goosebumps.

1440
01:03:17,630 --> 01:03:18,797
Thank you!

1441
01:03:19,933 --> 01:03:20,933
Awesome!

1442
01:03:21,901 --> 01:03:22,968
Amazing job.

1443
01:03:23,403 --> 01:03:26,405
We've got to be
near the top of the leaderboard...

1444
01:03:26,406 --> 01:03:28,307
for money made by just running.

1445
01:03:28,308 --> 01:03:29,942
Dollar for dollar, per kilometer,

1446
01:03:29,943 --> 01:03:31,543
we made more out there than Son Heung Min.

1447
01:03:31,778 --> 01:03:33,612
My chest was pounding out of my ribs.

1448
01:03:33,613 --> 01:03:35,981
My face was beet red.

1449
01:03:35,982 --> 01:03:38,250
I didn't care for a second
how I looked on camera.

1450
01:03:38,552 --> 01:03:40,352
When your head falls behind,
your legs have to make up for it.

1451
01:03:40,353 --> 01:03:41,887
Being in good physical shape...

1452
01:03:41,888 --> 01:03:43,755
can compensate for a lot.

1453
01:03:43,756 --> 01:03:45,924
When I was sprinting full tilt...

1454
01:03:45,925 --> 01:03:48,293
and clicked open that lockbox...

1455
01:03:48,294 --> 01:03:49,394
with the right key...

1456
01:03:49,395 --> 01:03:51,263
The US, seventh place. Unlocked!

1457
01:03:51,464 --> 01:03:53,432
"That's 20,000 dollars locked in!"

1458
01:03:53,433 --> 01:03:54,699
We conquered the US station.

1459
01:03:54,868 --> 01:03:56,368
While I was out there sprinting,
it hit me.

1460
01:03:56,369 --> 01:03:58,871
"What are we actually
running ourselves ragged for?"

1461
01:03:59,205 --> 01:04:00,839
Even mid-game,
something felt completely off.

1462
01:04:00,840 --> 01:04:03,409
The moment I tried to speak up,

1463
01:04:03,410 --> 01:04:05,577
it felt like they were freezing me out...

1464
01:04:05,578 --> 01:04:06,978
just like they did to Michael.

1465
01:04:06,979 --> 01:04:08,313
The cold shoulder was obvious.

1466
01:04:08,548 --> 01:04:09,882
Nangja, are you reading me?

1467
01:04:10,149 --> 01:04:11,082
Should I pretend I can't hear her?

1468
01:04:12,085 --> 01:04:13,452
And tell them I got lost!

1469
01:04:14,420 --> 01:04:16,521
I can't shake this doubt...

1470
01:04:16,522 --> 01:04:18,490
about whether this whole system
even works.

1471
01:04:18,491 --> 01:04:20,625
I stepped in with the Mexico crew...

1472
01:04:20,626 --> 01:04:22,527
and cleared every single box.

1473
01:04:22,528 --> 01:04:26,564
Then we moved over to Israel
and locked that down, too.

1474
01:04:27,133 --> 01:04:29,201
We've locked in their final ranking.

1475
01:04:29,202 --> 01:04:30,402
Israel is second.

1476
01:04:30,737 --> 01:04:31,603
The gender wage gap.

1477
01:04:32,272 --> 01:04:33,705
Pork consumption isn't zero.

1478
01:04:33,806 --> 01:04:35,740
- It definitely wasn't zero.
- Toss that key.

1479
01:04:35,741 --> 01:04:37,742
We grabbed all the symbols.

1480
01:04:37,743 --> 01:04:39,644
Now we just had to hustle
back to headquarters.

1481
01:04:40,750 --> 01:04:42,214
I felt fantastic,

1482
01:04:42,215 --> 01:04:44,416
knowing all that was left
was making it back to base.

1483
01:04:44,417 --> 01:04:46,251
Everyone busted their backs.

1484
01:04:46,252 --> 01:04:50,255
I just wish I could've pulled
more weight at the finish line.

1485
01:04:50,757 --> 01:04:52,324
- Hand over whatever keys you have left.
- Good hustle.

1486
01:04:53,960 --> 01:04:55,594
That's 80,000 dollars!

1487
01:04:55,595 --> 01:04:57,896
(All symbols collected,
securing 80,000 dollars)

1488
01:04:58,031 --> 01:04:59,932
You have five minutes remaining.

1489
01:05:00,600 --> 01:05:05,337
Discard every key except the five...

1490
01:05:05,338 --> 01:05:07,139
matching Korea's statistics.

1491
01:05:07,140 --> 01:05:08,774
Now we just have to weed out
the wrong keys.

1492
01:05:08,775 --> 01:05:09,941
If we nail this last part,
it's a clean sweep.

1493
01:05:09,942 --> 01:05:11,694
Average annual pork consumption
per capita.

1494
01:05:11,695 --> 01:05:12,945
It definitely isn't zero.

1495
01:05:12,946 --> 01:05:14,446
- Toss that one.
- Ditch it.

1496
01:05:14,447 --> 01:05:16,081
We're number one in the gender wage gap.

1497
01:05:16,082 --> 01:05:17,816
- First place.
- It can't be twelfth.

1498
01:05:17,817 --> 01:05:19,651
It's either 1L or 8L.

1499
01:05:19,652 --> 01:05:20,485
No way it's 1L.

1500
01:05:20,486 --> 01:05:22,087
What did Japan have?

1501
01:05:22,088 --> 01:05:23,188
Japan was 10.09L.

1502
01:05:23,189 --> 01:05:24,623
- Mexico was 5.05L.
- Japan, 10.09L.

1503
01:05:24,624 --> 01:05:25,958
Then Korea has to be...

1504
01:05:25,959 --> 01:05:27,292
right around 8L.

1505
01:05:27,293 --> 01:05:29,262
- Koreans love to drink and sing.
- Israel is 4L.

1506
01:05:29,263 --> 01:05:31,029
- We definitely drink more than that.
- No question.

1507
01:05:31,030 --> 01:05:32,097
No way they beat us on that.

1508
01:05:32,098 --> 01:05:33,566
Our life expectancy has to be much higher.

1509
01:05:33,567 --> 01:05:35,133
As far as I know, it's in the low 80s.

1510
01:05:35,134 --> 01:05:37,120
- Definitely not 92.
- No way.

1511
01:05:37,870 --> 01:05:39,171
We could actually walk away
with 100,000 dollars.

1512
01:05:39,172 --> 01:05:40,205
Last one: annual working hours.

1513
01:05:40,206 --> 01:05:41,139
- Working hours.
- Working hours.

1514
01:05:41,140 --> 01:05:42,642
It's down to 1,901 versus 2,248.

1515
01:05:42,643 --> 01:05:44,910
Even with the statutory 52-hour workweek,

1516
01:05:45,178 --> 01:05:47,245
that still clocks in
around 2,400 hours a year.

1517
01:05:47,246 --> 01:05:49,414
I really don't think it's 1,901.

1518
01:05:49,415 --> 01:05:51,750
That's way below our statutory baseline.

1519
01:05:51,751 --> 01:05:53,619
Just so you know,

1520
01:05:53,620 --> 01:05:55,754
this is the highest figure on the board.

1521
01:05:55,989 --> 01:05:57,957
The US is sitting at 1,811 hours.

1522
01:05:58,357 --> 01:06:00,059
I'm torn.

1523
01:06:00,060 --> 01:06:02,294
If we work 8 hours a day, 5 days a week,

1524
01:06:02,295 --> 01:06:03,595
that comes out to 1,920 hours.

1525
01:06:03,596 --> 01:06:05,964
We're notorious for brutal overtime
because we log...

1526
01:06:05,965 --> 01:06:07,165
way more hours than other OECD countries.

1527
01:06:07,266 --> 01:06:09,367
Compared to other nations,
Korea's hours are notoriously long.

1528
01:06:09,368 --> 01:06:10,368
- We work crazy hours.
- Exactly.

1529
01:06:10,770 --> 01:06:12,371
- Let's lock this one in.
- Let's go with this.

1530
01:06:12,372 --> 01:06:13,472
We've already banked 80,000 dollars
anyway.

1531
01:06:13,473 --> 01:06:16,375
- It seems to be based...
- Let's do it!

1532
01:06:16,376 --> 01:06:17,876
- Lock it in!
- Lock it in!

1533
01:06:17,877 --> 01:06:18,911
What if we blew it?

1534
01:06:18,912 --> 01:06:20,212
Worst case,
we still walk away with 80,000 dollars.

1535
01:06:20,213 --> 01:06:22,114
- Still, 80,000 dollars is guaranteed.
- Exactly...

1536
01:06:25,953 --> 01:06:26,818
Please be right.

1537
01:06:26,986 --> 01:06:28,153
Please.

1538
01:06:28,354 --> 01:06:30,355
Time is up.

1539
01:06:30,923 --> 01:06:32,757
My heart is racing.

1540
01:06:32,758 --> 01:06:33,758
I'm so stressed.

1541
01:06:34,060 --> 01:06:34,860
I can't watch.

1542
01:06:35,707 --> 01:06:38,742
Today's total earnings come out...

1543
01:06:39,465 --> 01:06:41,733
to 80,000 dollars.

1544
01:06:42,435 --> 01:06:43,802
We won 80,000 dollars!

1545
01:06:44,270 --> 01:06:45,804
- We earned 80,000 dollars!
- We brought home 80K!

1546
01:06:46,774 --> 01:06:48,957
- Which one did we miss?
- The question you missed was...

1547
01:06:48,958 --> 01:06:50,175
working hours.

1548
01:06:50,176 --> 01:06:51,643
So it really was 1,901.

1549
01:06:51,644 --> 01:06:52,878
(Average annual hours worked per worker:
Korea, 1,901 hours)

1550
01:06:52,879 --> 01:06:54,513
Korea's working hours...

1551
01:06:54,714 --> 01:06:57,049
- aren't as high as we thought.
- I'm so sorry.

1552
01:06:57,050 --> 01:06:58,150
I blew that one. I'm sorry.

1553
01:06:58,151 --> 01:06:59,051
- Don't sweat it.
- It's totally fine.

1554
01:06:59,052 --> 01:07:01,553
- Don't apologize.
- You're good! We crushed it.

1555
01:07:01,554 --> 01:07:03,522
- We did amazing out there.
- Look how much we made!

1556
01:07:03,523 --> 01:07:05,490
- Great job, everyone.
- We did incredible.

1557
01:07:05,491 --> 01:07:08,226
We made seven or eight times
what we made yesterday.

1558
01:07:08,227 --> 01:07:09,528
That's massive money.

1559
01:07:09,529 --> 01:07:11,630
I really should've known that.

1560
01:07:11,631 --> 01:07:12,864
- We're super proud.
- Outstanding work.

1561
01:07:13,232 --> 01:07:16,167
I felt terrible for blowing
that final call.

1562
01:07:16,168 --> 01:07:18,336
One thing that weighed on my mind was...

1563
01:07:18,337 --> 01:07:20,471
people feeling let down...

1564
01:07:20,472 --> 01:07:22,006
if we only brought back 80,000 dollars...

1565
01:07:22,007 --> 01:07:23,775
after hyping up
the 100,000-dollar jackpot.

1566
01:07:23,876 --> 01:07:25,577
But honestly,

1567
01:07:25,578 --> 01:07:28,513
whether it was 80 grand or 100 grand,
it was still a huge haul.

1568
01:07:28,514 --> 01:07:30,181
That was what was eating at me.

1569
01:07:30,182 --> 01:07:31,617
We left 20,000 dollars on the table...

1570
01:07:31,618 --> 01:07:34,953
just because we couldn't nail
that last key.

1571
01:07:34,954 --> 01:07:36,355
Truth is, I think I knew the answer.

1572
01:07:36,356 --> 01:07:38,323
I remember reading it in the paper
not too long ago.

1573
01:07:38,324 --> 01:07:40,693
But with everyone already
eyeing me suspiciously,

1574
01:07:40,694 --> 01:07:43,161
I chose to lay low and keep my mouth shut.

1575
01:07:43,505 --> 01:07:45,940
If I had spoken up
and claimed the full 100,000 dollars,

1576
01:07:45,965 --> 01:07:47,265
I would've openly challenged the group.

1577
01:07:47,266 --> 01:07:49,767
"Why are we splitting this down the middle
with the people who stayed behind?"

1578
01:07:49,768 --> 01:07:51,669
I wanted to push that debate.

1579
01:07:51,670 --> 01:07:54,772
Splitting it right down
the middle didn't feel right.

1580
01:07:56,342 --> 01:07:58,176
Let's figure out the prize split first,

1581
01:07:58,578 --> 01:08:01,313
then we can talk taxes.

1582
01:08:01,547 --> 01:08:02,747
So we're working with 80,000 dollars.

1583
01:08:03,116 --> 01:08:05,016
There are 12 of us total.

1584
01:08:05,017 --> 01:08:07,119
I think we should distribute
at least 60,000 dollars...

1585
01:08:07,120 --> 01:08:08,721
- across the board.
- Agreed.

1586
01:08:08,988 --> 01:08:10,890
If we split 60,000 dollars 12 ways,
everyone gets 5,000 dollars.

1587
01:08:10,891 --> 01:08:12,390
Then we can divide
the remaining 20,000 among us.

1588
01:08:12,391 --> 01:08:13,925
Distribute 60,000 dollars.

1589
01:08:13,926 --> 01:08:14,759
That's 5,000 dollars a head.

1590
01:08:14,760 --> 01:08:17,128
Since 60,000 divides cleanly by 12,

1591
01:08:17,129 --> 01:08:18,530
- let's lock that baseline in.
- Sounds good.

1592
01:08:18,531 --> 01:08:22,133
We definitely earned a performance bonus.

1593
01:08:22,134 --> 01:08:26,171
If we divide the remaining 20,000 by 6,
that's roughly 3,000 each.

1594
01:08:26,172 --> 01:08:27,572
That leaves 2,000 left over.

1595
01:08:27,573 --> 01:08:29,140
So 3,000 each, plus 2,000 in reserve.

1596
01:08:29,141 --> 01:08:32,711
Is everyone okay with us claiming
a 30,000-dollar incentive pool?

1597
01:08:32,712 --> 01:08:34,079
Honestly...

1598
01:08:36,048 --> 01:08:37,916
I'd be satisfied
with an extra 1,000 dollars.

1599
01:08:37,917 --> 01:08:41,734
Same here. Especially
since this whole plan was partly my idea.

1600
01:08:41,735 --> 01:08:44,963
Yesterday we had that disaster
where everyone took a 2,000-dollar hit...

1601
01:08:44,964 --> 01:08:46,657
from the Ideology Checking penalty.

1602
01:08:47,527 --> 01:08:49,829
Maybe we cushion the blow
for the rest of the house...

1603
01:08:49,830 --> 01:08:52,197
by dialing back our cut.

1604
01:08:52,198 --> 01:08:54,633
What if we take less
than 3,000 dollars each?

1605
01:08:54,634 --> 01:08:56,768
I think that's fair.

1606
01:08:56,769 --> 01:08:59,404
- That leaves 8,000.
- Right, 8,000 left over.

1607
01:08:59,405 --> 01:09:02,029
- Divide that among the 6 of us,
- and that's 1,000 dollars each.

1608
01:09:02,030 --> 01:09:03,876
Which still leaves
2,000 dollars on the table.

1609
01:09:03,877 --> 01:09:06,011
Two of us could take
an extra 1,000 dollars.

1610
01:09:06,012 --> 01:09:07,679
Why don't we let our leader make the call?

1611
01:09:07,780 --> 01:09:08,680
Let's just draw lots.

1612
01:09:09,081 --> 01:09:10,415
- Fair enough.
- Sounds good.

1613
01:09:11,317 --> 01:09:12,651
(Drawing lots to see who gets
the remaining 2,000 dollars)

1614
01:09:12,652 --> 01:09:13,885
Here we go.

1615
01:09:13,886 --> 01:09:15,787
Wait. 1, 2... Okay!

1616
01:09:16,123 --> 01:09:17,089
Blank.

1617
01:09:18,157 --> 01:09:19,023
I got it!

1618
01:09:19,024 --> 01:09:20,458
- Blank slip.
- Congrats!

1619
01:09:20,459 --> 01:09:21,459
I won!

1620
01:09:22,695 --> 01:09:23,995
I never win these things!

1621
01:09:23,996 --> 01:09:25,597
So who pulled the winners?

1622
01:09:26,365 --> 01:09:27,832
Ted.

1623
01:09:27,833 --> 01:09:29,067
(Ted and Hippo receive
8,000 dollars each.)

1624
01:09:29,068 --> 01:09:30,335
- Hippo.
- Hippo got it.

1625
01:09:31,370 --> 01:09:33,338
I kind of wish we could've just done
500 dollars each.

1626
01:09:34,540 --> 01:09:36,841
Honestly, it was so rough out there.

1627
01:09:37,643 --> 01:09:41,779
Deep down, I wasn't thrilled
about sharing it equally.

1628
01:09:43,082 --> 01:09:45,550
Logically, I got why we did it,

1629
01:09:45,551 --> 01:09:48,520
but after running until my legs gave out,
it stung a little.

1630
01:09:49,755 --> 01:09:51,355
Part of me felt entitled to a bigger cut.

1631
01:09:51,356 --> 01:09:53,758
I'm sure everyone on the field crew felt
that sting.

1632
01:09:54,260 --> 01:09:56,261
But once you start going down that road,

1633
01:09:56,262 --> 01:09:58,129
there's no stopping it.

1634
01:09:58,297 --> 01:10:00,932
When big money is on the line,

1635
01:10:00,933 --> 01:10:04,569
greed takes over and turns people
against each other.

1636
01:10:04,570 --> 01:10:06,704
That's why I deliberately pushed...

1637
01:10:06,705 --> 01:10:10,275
for a more balanced split.

1638
01:10:11,077 --> 01:10:14,579
(Every resident receives a 6,000-dollar
baseline payout.)

1639
01:10:14,580 --> 01:10:16,682
So, everyone was given 6,000 dollars each.

1640
01:10:17,550 --> 01:10:18,817
That's insane money!

1641
01:10:18,818 --> 01:10:20,552
Tell me about it! We're loaded!

1642
01:10:20,553 --> 01:10:21,753
I can finally breathe easy.

1643
01:10:21,754 --> 01:10:22,988
Let's get some food ordered first.

1644
01:10:22,989 --> 01:10:24,189
- Yes, please.
- Food first, definitely.

1645
01:10:24,190 --> 01:10:25,657
- Let's order dinner.
- What are we getting?

1646
01:10:25,658 --> 01:10:26,858
I'm down for whatever.

1647
01:10:26,859 --> 01:10:28,193
One meal kit.

1648
01:10:28,461 --> 01:10:31,196
- One meal kit and one grocery pack.
- One grocery pack, got it.

1649
01:10:31,564 --> 01:10:33,131
Your purchase is confirmed.

1650
01:10:33,499 --> 01:10:34,766
The cost will be debited
from the community fund.

1651
01:10:35,034 --> 01:10:40,105
(Prize money distribution
and fee deduction start.)

1652
01:10:41,273 --> 01:10:42,306
That accounts for the full 80,000 dollars.

1653
01:10:42,307 --> 01:10:44,671
(Stay-behind crew: 6,000 dollars,
Ted, Hippo: 8,000 dollars)

1654
01:10:44,672 --> 01:10:46,878
(Go Ae Shin, Nangja, Superman, Genie:
7,000 dollars)

1655
01:10:46,879 --> 01:10:48,980
Here is the final breakdown.

1656
01:10:48,981 --> 01:10:49,881
(Prize money distribution complete.)

1657
01:10:50,074 --> 01:10:51,274
And our community tax rate is...

1658
01:10:51,851 --> 01:10:53,920
(Ten percent tax deduction applied)

1659
01:10:53,921 --> 01:10:56,521
Ten percent flat.

1660
01:10:56,522 --> 01:11:02,096
(Day Three Ideology Check
Failure Penalties)

1661
01:11:02,328 --> 01:11:06,338
(Day Four Prize Money Distribution)

1662
01:11:07,133 --> 01:11:11,843
(Community fund deduction)

1663
01:11:11,843 --> 01:11:14,639
(1,000 dollars added
to leader's personal account)

1664
01:11:17,844 --> 01:11:21,680
(Community fund collection complete.)

1665
01:11:21,681 --> 01:11:24,015
The leader has distributed
the prize money...

1666
01:11:24,318 --> 01:11:27,520
and collected ten percent
of each resident's personal funds...

1667
01:11:27,521 --> 01:11:29,388
for the community chest.

1668
01:11:29,655 --> 01:11:30,388
The total will update shortly.

1669
01:11:30,389 --> 01:11:33,591
You can check your remaining balance...

1670
01:11:33,793 --> 01:11:37,028
and tax deduction in your personal rooms.

1671
01:11:37,029 --> 01:11:38,397
We really backed the right leader!

1672
01:11:38,965 --> 01:11:41,266
You promised you'd bring in the big bucks,
and you totally delivered!

1673
01:11:41,267 --> 01:11:43,702
I can't believe our balance is
at 15,000 dollars.

1674
01:11:44,170 --> 01:11:47,005
Gosh, 15,000 dollars in the bank!

1675
01:11:47,006 --> 01:11:48,843
We were joking about craving
black bean noodles yesterday,

1676
01:11:48,844 --> 01:11:50,268
and now we can actually afford it.

1677
01:11:50,943 --> 01:11:53,011
Should we have just ordered
the black bean noodles?

1678
01:11:53,012 --> 01:11:55,413
That's 12,000 dollars right there.

1679
01:11:55,414 --> 01:11:56,847
One bowl is 1,000 dollars.

1680
01:11:57,350 --> 01:11:59,217
I just checked the menu.

1681
01:11:59,218 --> 01:12:02,054
Every single prepared dish is
1,000 dollars.

1682
01:12:02,055 --> 01:12:04,523
- For real?
- It explicitly says "per dish."

1683
01:12:04,724 --> 01:12:07,159
You're right. That's insane.

1684
01:12:07,793 --> 01:12:10,428
Wait, so a single bowl of jjajangmyeon
is 1,000 dollars?

1685
01:12:10,429 --> 01:12:11,796
Looks like it.

1686
01:12:11,997 --> 01:12:13,998
We'd need a 100,000-dollar bankroll
to eat like that.

1687
01:12:14,133 --> 01:12:15,900
The more money you have,
the tighter you squeeze every penny.

1688
01:12:15,901 --> 01:12:18,336
When you're broke,
you crave expensive takeout.

1689
01:12:18,337 --> 01:12:19,571
But once the cash is in your hand...

1690
01:12:20,139 --> 01:12:21,206
"We broke our backs for this money!"

1691
01:12:21,207 --> 01:12:23,141
After sweating bullets out there,

1692
01:12:23,142 --> 01:12:24,576
it suddenly hurts...

1693
01:12:24,577 --> 01:12:25,910
- to spend it.
- Exactly.

1694
01:12:26,178 --> 01:12:27,746
Now the whole game is...

1695
01:12:27,747 --> 01:12:29,181
protecting the bag.

1696
01:12:30,883 --> 01:12:33,218
Could you add a Coke Zero for me?

1697
01:12:33,219 --> 01:12:34,519
- Do they sell soda?
- Soda?

1698
01:12:34,687 --> 01:12:36,123
Me too. Put me down...

1699
01:12:36,124 --> 01:12:37,288
- for a Coke Zero as well.
- Coke Zero, got it.

1700
01:12:37,289 --> 01:12:39,457
I'll take a Coke Zero over booze tonight.

1701
01:12:40,393 --> 01:12:42,094
Twelve cans of beer.

1702
01:12:42,360 --> 01:12:43,694
Three bottles of soju.

1703
01:12:44,063 --> 01:12:46,631
Five Coke Zeros.

1704
01:12:46,932 --> 01:12:48,933
One loaf of bread, cereal, and jam.

1705
01:12:48,934 --> 01:12:50,434
Your order has been placed.

1706
01:12:50,770 --> 01:12:52,237
The total will be charged
to the community fund.

1707
01:12:52,438 --> 01:12:54,539
Even after placing that order,
we're still sitting on over 10 grand.

1708
01:12:54,540 --> 01:12:56,541
- It's a straight-up miracle.
- It really is.

1709
01:12:56,642 --> 01:12:58,009
We ordered all that food,

1710
01:12:58,010 --> 01:12:59,611
and our balance is still
at 11,000 dollars.

1711
01:13:00,613 --> 01:13:01,647
That was a huge haul.

1712
01:13:01,648 --> 01:13:03,682
We went on a shopping spree,
and we're still over 10,000 dollars.

1713
01:13:04,417 --> 01:13:05,851
We even treated ourselves to Coke Zero.

1714
01:13:06,619 --> 01:13:07,852
I bought a total luxury item.

1715
01:13:07,853 --> 01:13:09,353
We literally pulled ourselves
out of poverty...

1716
01:13:09,354 --> 01:13:11,722
- and joined the developed world.
- We did.

1717
01:13:11,891 --> 01:13:13,758
Full-on developed nation status.

1718
01:13:16,296 --> 01:13:21,032
Money builds a safety net
and makes life comfortable,

1719
01:13:22,635 --> 01:13:27,096
but in a capitalist society,
it also dictates raw power and class.

1720
01:13:36,348 --> 01:13:39,550
Even the players who had a zero balance...

1721
01:13:39,551 --> 01:13:40,985
are now sitting on at least 5,400 dollars.

1722
01:13:42,254 --> 01:13:43,721
A cool 5,400 dollars.

1723
01:13:45,558 --> 01:13:46,490
Man.

1724
01:13:48,060 --> 01:13:50,061
Last night, the house...

1725
01:13:50,630 --> 01:13:53,031
got into a conversation about money.

1726
01:13:54,133 --> 01:13:57,068
(Night of Day Three)

1727
01:13:59,505 --> 01:14:01,606
(Resident Annual Salaries)

1728
01:14:01,607 --> 01:14:04,376
The following is a breakdown
of resident annual salaries.

1729
01:14:04,944 --> 01:14:06,411
- Annual salaries?
- They're showing salaries?

1730
01:14:06,879 --> 01:14:08,580
Looks like we're diving
into social class today.

1731
01:14:08,581 --> 01:14:10,081
Today's topic is class.

1732
01:14:16,188 --> 01:14:18,356
Under 50,000 dollars: 1 resident.

1733
01:14:18,858 --> 01:14:21,960
Over 50,000 and up to 100,000 dollars:
6 residents.

1734
01:14:22,728 --> 01:14:25,930
Over 100,000 and up to 200,000 dollars:
3 residents.

1735
01:14:26,225 --> 01:14:29,327
Over 200,000 and up to 300,000 dollars:
1 resident.

1736
01:14:29,768 --> 01:14:31,869
Over 300,000 dollars: 1 resident.

1737
01:14:32,238 --> 01:14:34,773
- Over 300,000 dollars?
- Who makes that much?

1738
01:14:34,774 --> 01:14:35,640
Check out the top bracket.

1739
01:14:35,641 --> 01:14:37,008
Show us 500,000 dollars!

1740
01:14:39,044 --> 01:14:42,146
I found it bizarre how people cheered
for the highest brackets.

1741
01:14:42,449 --> 01:14:47,052
If we applaud someone making
over 300,000 dollars a year,

1742
01:14:47,286 --> 01:14:49,053
does that mean...

1743
01:14:50,055 --> 01:14:51,789
someone making
under 50,000 dollars is doing bad?

1744
01:14:51,857 --> 01:14:56,027
Does earning more money make you
a better human being,

1745
01:14:56,162 --> 01:14:58,362
or someone more deserving of applause?

1746
01:14:58,363 --> 01:15:00,531
I don't understand why people treat that
as an unwritten rule.

1747
01:15:00,866 --> 01:15:03,367
When we shared
which presidents we admired,

1748
01:15:03,669 --> 01:15:07,171
nobody cheered for the one
with the most votes.

1749
01:15:07,172 --> 01:15:09,574
It made me realize
how conditioned people really are.

1750
01:15:09,775 --> 01:15:10,908
I didn't cheer.

1751
01:15:11,076 --> 01:15:12,643
I was honestly just floored...

1752
01:15:12,644 --> 01:15:16,313
by how many top earners were in this room.

1753
01:15:16,582 --> 01:15:20,184
(Day Three debate: Class)

1754
01:15:20,792 --> 01:15:23,460
Today's topic is class.

1755
01:15:24,390 --> 01:15:27,258
The debate proposition is as follows:

1756
01:15:28,427 --> 01:15:32,463
"Individuals are primarily responsible
for their own poverty."

1757
01:15:33,933 --> 01:15:35,300
(Affirmative: Go Ae Shin)

1758
01:15:35,301 --> 01:15:37,102
(Dark Knight)

1759
01:15:37,103 --> 01:15:40,906
(Superman)

1760
01:15:40,907 --> 01:15:42,274
(Negative: Benjamin)

1761
01:15:42,275 --> 01:15:44,242
(Polar Bear)

1762
01:15:44,243 --> 01:15:45,443
(Ted)

1763
01:15:48,781 --> 01:15:50,149
To be completely honest,

1764
01:15:50,150 --> 01:15:53,284
the debate was so agonizing to watch that
I turned the screen off midway through.

1765
01:15:53,452 --> 01:15:55,520
It felt like nobody in that room
actually grasped what poverty means.

1766
01:15:55,821 --> 01:15:58,523
It's such a heavy topic,
especially dropped right after...

1767
01:15:58,524 --> 01:16:00,491
everyone's salaries were put on blast.

1768
01:16:00,759 --> 01:16:03,027
(In modern capitalism,)

1769
01:16:03,028 --> 01:16:06,831
(everyone competes on open ground,
meaning individuals must...)

1770
01:16:06,832 --> 01:16:12,636
(take responsibility for
how their lives pan out.)

1771
01:16:12,637 --> 01:16:14,605
(Even in restrictive conditions,)

1772
01:16:14,606 --> 01:16:16,741
(people can always cultivate
their skills...)

1773
01:16:16,742 --> 01:16:19,877
(and lift themselves up
through grit and ability.)

1774
01:16:19,878 --> 01:16:21,545
(Just as generational wealth exists,)

1775
01:16:21,546 --> 01:16:23,748
(generational poverty is a reality.)

1776
01:16:23,749 --> 01:16:26,973
(Some people are born into circumstances
with zero access...)

1777
01:16:26,974 --> 01:16:30,087
(to basic opportunities or networks.)

1778
01:16:30,088 --> 01:16:31,822
(Poverty is undeniably shaped...)

1779
01:16:31,823 --> 01:16:34,125
(by systemic and structural barriers.)

1780
01:16:34,126 --> 01:16:36,460
(You wouldn't look at severe poverty
in developing nations...)

1781
01:16:36,461 --> 01:16:38,964
(and claim it exists
because their ancestors...)

1782
01:16:38,965 --> 01:16:43,134
(simply didn't work hard enough.)

1783
01:16:46,838 --> 01:16:50,274
(To the Affirmative: how do you account
for the widening wealth gap...)

1784
01:16:50,275 --> 01:16:52,510
(driven by inherited capital...)

1785
01:16:52,511 --> 01:16:54,284
(and generational class immobility?)

1786
01:16:54,285 --> 01:16:56,448
(These are fundamental components...)

1787
01:16:56,449 --> 01:17:01,085
(of systemic and structural failure.)

1788
01:17:01,086 --> 01:17:03,854
("Class status is inherited
across generations.")

1789
01:17:03,855 --> 01:17:06,286
("The wealth gap continues to widen."
Even granting those points,)

1790
01:17:06,287 --> 01:17:08,559
(one cannot reduce poverty entirely
to societal fault.)

1791
01:17:08,560 --> 01:17:11,595
(In liberal democracies,)

1792
01:17:11,596 --> 01:17:14,440
(individual poverty cannot simply
be blamed on society.)

1793
01:17:18,504 --> 01:17:21,506
They look down at someone
who's down and out...

1794
01:17:21,507 --> 01:17:24,576
with this patronizing brand
of pity, thinking,

1795
01:17:24,710 --> 01:17:27,647
"With all these social safety nets,
why can't they just pull themselves up?"

1796
01:17:27,648 --> 01:17:31,115
"They must just be lazy."

1797
01:17:31,116 --> 01:17:34,485
Where do you even begin with people
who have lived that insulated a life?

1798
01:17:35,054 --> 01:17:37,956
I felt like I was screaming
at a brick wall.

1799
01:17:38,923 --> 01:17:42,593
I stand by the belief
that individuals own their poverty.

1800
01:17:42,594 --> 01:17:46,363
Most of them weren't speaking
from lived experience.

1801
01:17:47,066 --> 01:17:48,800
They were parroting talking points...

1802
01:17:49,001 --> 01:17:50,802
they picked up secondhand.

1803
01:17:51,070 --> 01:17:52,951
It blows my mind
how casually people can pass off...

1804
01:17:53,038 --> 01:17:55,139
secondhand theories
as their own convictions.

1805
01:17:55,374 --> 01:17:57,075
I put everything I had into it.

1806
01:17:57,076 --> 01:17:58,776
I clawed my way up from the bottom...

1807
01:17:58,777 --> 01:18:01,746
without a handout from anyone.

1808
01:18:03,215 --> 01:18:04,479
Watching that debate made me cringe.

1809
01:18:04,480 --> 01:18:06,984
I felt that exact same embarrassment
when the salaries popped up.

1810
01:18:07,419 --> 01:18:09,387
"Do you all really think life is
this effortless?"

1811
01:18:09,388 --> 01:18:12,823
"Did you coast into money
with that kind of naive outlook?"

1812
01:18:13,259 --> 01:18:15,559
Watching them throw jabs...

1813
01:18:15,560 --> 01:18:17,695
from that smug, high-and-mighty perch...

1814
01:18:17,696 --> 01:18:20,798
acting like they had the entire world
all figured out.

1815
01:18:20,866 --> 01:18:23,267
"How would someone
who has actually lived through poverty..."

1816
01:18:23,268 --> 01:18:25,069
"feel watching this circus?"

1817
01:18:25,638 --> 01:18:27,405
After the debate,
a post went up on the Community feed.

1818
01:18:27,406 --> 01:18:29,273
That really made me stop and reflect.

1819
01:18:29,274 --> 01:18:30,241
The author clearly...

1820
01:18:30,876 --> 01:18:32,009
had some kind of...

1821
01:18:32,010 --> 01:18:34,712
real, firsthand exposure to poverty.

1822
01:18:35,721 --> 01:18:37,481
I posted that on the feed.

1823
01:18:37,750 --> 01:18:41,086
I felt like someone needed to say it.

1824
01:18:42,221 --> 01:18:45,523
The most infuriating thing
about public debates on poverty...

1825
01:18:45,824 --> 01:18:48,342
is that they're almost
always spearheaded...

1826
01:18:48,343 --> 01:18:52,029
by people who have never known
a single day of real lack.

1827
01:18:54,032 --> 01:18:56,433
"Why can't you escape poverty..."

1828
01:18:56,434 --> 01:18:59,937
"when the welfare state is so developed?"

1829
01:18:59,938 --> 01:19:02,473
Because people don't even know
those programs exist.

1830
01:19:03,042 --> 01:19:05,009
Because they can't take time off...

1831
01:19:05,010 --> 01:19:07,378
to visit a municipal office
during regular business hours.

1832
01:19:07,880 --> 01:19:09,481
Because their credit score is shot.

1833
01:19:09,682 --> 01:19:12,917
Because they don't have a working laptop
or internet access...

1834
01:19:13,318 --> 01:19:14,918
to apply for those benefits.

1835
01:19:15,321 --> 01:19:17,989
There are countless invisible barriers.

1836
01:19:18,723 --> 01:19:20,191
Poverty isn't just...

1837
01:19:20,192 --> 01:19:23,194
a lack of material possessions.

1838
01:19:23,662 --> 01:19:27,431
Poverty suffocates a person's potential...

1839
01:19:27,666 --> 01:19:31,268
in layers upon layers of unseen damage.

1840
01:19:32,704 --> 01:19:37,074
The worst part is
losing belief in yourself.

1841
01:19:39,511 --> 01:19:41,145
Watching that debate made one
thing painfully clear.

1842
01:19:41,146 --> 01:19:43,782
"So many people harbor outright
disgust toward poverty."

1843
01:19:44,316 --> 01:19:46,666
Is there anyone in that room have
actually crawled out of the dirt...

1844
01:19:46,667 --> 01:19:48,084
and made something of themselves?

1845
01:19:50,756 --> 01:19:51,725
(Pre-show interview)

1846
01:19:51,726 --> 01:19:53,458
Is this the spot?

1847
01:19:54,960 --> 01:19:56,794
Do I look into the lens
or look at you guys?

1848
01:19:56,996 --> 01:20:01,732
Growing up, my family was strapped
for cash.

1849
01:20:01,900 --> 01:20:04,937
I grew up knowing that a single misstep...

1850
01:20:04,938 --> 01:20:08,639
carried a massive opportunity cost.

1851
01:20:08,664 --> 01:20:11,866
Because we didn't have
a financial cushion,

1852
01:20:12,177 --> 01:20:14,097
whenever I sank my own money
into trying something new...

1853
01:20:14,098 --> 01:20:15,312
and came up short,

1854
01:20:15,447 --> 01:20:19,450
it felt like I had plunged straight
into debt just for taking the shot.

1855
01:20:19,451 --> 01:20:21,485
When I finally became an adult,
the single greatest feeling...

1856
01:20:21,486 --> 01:20:23,053
was the realization
that I could earn my own living.

1857
01:20:23,155 --> 01:20:25,423
When I was 20,

1858
01:20:25,424 --> 01:20:26,991
I worked three part-time jobs.

1859
01:20:26,992 --> 01:20:28,926
I worked at a cram school in the morning,

1860
01:20:29,093 --> 01:20:30,294
then at a bakery,

1861
01:20:30,529 --> 01:20:33,764
and I even took on data entry gigs
on the side.

1862
01:20:34,066 --> 01:20:37,101
I'd work on my feet all day...

1863
01:20:37,102 --> 01:20:38,803
and then pull all-nighters
cramming for exams.

1864
01:20:39,071 --> 01:20:40,872
It was exhausting.

1865
01:20:41,240 --> 01:20:42,774
Meanwhile, my friends had peace of mind.

1866
01:20:43,709 --> 01:20:47,145
They could sit comfortably
in the library...

1867
01:20:47,146 --> 01:20:50,481
focusing solely on their coursework.

1868
01:20:51,116 --> 01:20:53,217
I constantly felt like
I was pouring water...

1869
01:20:53,218 --> 01:20:54,518
into a broken bucket.

1870
01:20:54,519 --> 01:20:56,020
No matter how hard I ground,

1871
01:20:56,021 --> 01:20:58,890
there was never any money left
at the end of the month.

1872
01:20:58,891 --> 01:21:01,425
I remember thinking,

1873
01:21:01,426 --> 01:21:03,794
"There's no way this is normal."

1874
01:21:04,095 --> 01:21:05,930
I started questioning the disparities...

1875
01:21:05,931 --> 01:21:07,632
that everyone else seemed
to take for granted.

1876
01:21:08,166 --> 01:21:09,900
I grew up during the IMF crisis.

1877
01:21:10,035 --> 01:21:12,036
When childhood is like that,

1878
01:21:12,437 --> 01:21:13,504
you have to grow up fast.

1879
01:21:13,839 --> 01:21:15,173
Back in high school,

1880
01:21:15,574 --> 01:21:17,575
I applied to the Korea Military Academy.

1881
01:21:18,043 --> 01:21:19,577
It wasn't out of some grand sense
of duty...

1882
01:21:20,012 --> 01:21:22,012
or a lifelong dream
of wearing the uniform.

1883
01:21:22,547 --> 01:21:23,981
It was simply because tuition was free.

1884
01:21:24,182 --> 01:21:26,316
They told me my education would
be fully covered through graduation...

1885
01:21:26,884 --> 01:21:28,486
and I'd even draw a salary.

1886
01:21:28,487 --> 01:21:30,154
Knowing I'd commission
as a career officer afterward,

1887
01:21:30,356 --> 01:21:31,722
I saw it as guaranteed stability...

1888
01:21:31,723 --> 01:21:33,657
and a real way to take the burden
off my parents.

1889
01:21:34,359 --> 01:21:37,061
I made it all the way
through graduate school.

1890
01:21:37,462 --> 01:21:40,264
I'm the only one in my entire family
with a college degree.

1891
01:21:40,565 --> 01:21:42,733
Neither of my parents
ever had the chance...

1892
01:21:42,734 --> 01:21:44,601
to even finish elementary school properly.

1893
01:21:44,803 --> 01:21:48,139
I grew up running around
the tiny spicy rice cake stand...

1894
01:21:48,140 --> 01:21:50,406
and print shop my parents ran...

1895
01:21:50,407 --> 01:21:52,510
in the market.

1896
01:21:53,012 --> 01:21:55,846
Even to this day,
I can't ride a bicycle...

1897
01:21:56,280 --> 01:21:57,948
because nobody ever taught me how.

1898
01:21:58,317 --> 01:22:01,519
In my household, anything that
wasn't about basic survival...

1899
01:22:01,520 --> 01:22:02,820
felt like an indulgence.

1900
01:22:03,121 --> 01:22:04,688
Once I got to college,

1901
01:22:04,689 --> 01:22:06,390
the divide between me and everyone else
hit me even harder.

1902
01:22:06,658 --> 01:22:09,827
It was a cultural and intellectual gulf.

1903
01:22:10,128 --> 01:22:11,995
My parents had spent their whole lives
just trying to make ends meet,

1904
01:22:11,996 --> 01:22:16,201
while other people grew up surrounded
by things to enjoy.

1905
01:22:16,202 --> 01:22:18,836
Personal taste, an eye for design,

1906
01:22:19,204 --> 01:22:20,673
and all those
unspoken little privileges...

1907
01:22:20,674 --> 01:22:23,007
that smooth out life
and make it comfortable.

1908
01:22:23,408 --> 01:22:25,075
People leverage those cultural markers...

1909
01:22:25,577 --> 01:22:28,412
as social capital...

1910
01:22:29,081 --> 01:22:32,416
and quietly look down
on anyone without them.

1911
01:22:33,452 --> 01:22:37,021
I felt that to my core in college.

1912
01:22:37,756 --> 01:22:40,491
It was so isolating...

1913
01:22:40,792 --> 01:22:42,093
because no one felt like they came
from my world.

1914
01:22:42,336 --> 01:22:45,063
Even if someone did, they were probably
keeping it hidden, just like me.

1915
01:22:45,651 --> 01:22:47,464
Our place always had rats.

1916
01:22:48,006 --> 01:22:49,573
Growing up,

1917
01:22:50,202 --> 01:22:52,770
all six of us were crammed
into a single room.

1918
01:22:53,338 --> 01:22:54,404
When I was looking at military enlistment,

1919
01:22:54,405 --> 01:22:56,173
the first thing that terrified me was
the 100-day leave.

1920
01:22:56,409 --> 01:22:58,476
Guys with financial support from home...

1921
01:22:58,477 --> 01:23:00,678
would get some pocket money
from their parents...

1922
01:23:01,013 --> 01:23:03,114
to go out, treat their friends,

1923
01:23:03,115 --> 01:23:04,882
or take their girlfriends somewhere nice.

1924
01:23:04,883 --> 01:23:06,584
I couldn't even bring myself
to ask my mom...

1925
01:23:07,085 --> 01:23:08,685
for some money.

1926
01:23:08,920 --> 01:23:11,221
I would've had to pick up
three shifts a week just to get by...

1927
01:23:11,222 --> 01:23:12,923
and only hang out with friends...

1928
01:23:12,924 --> 01:23:14,825
if there were any scraps left over.

1929
01:23:16,561 --> 01:23:17,836
I decided to go into the military...

1930
01:23:17,837 --> 01:23:20,425
because the thought of being too
broke to enjoy basic leave broke me.

1931
01:23:23,668 --> 01:23:25,068
It wasn't until I joined this project...

1932
01:23:25,069 --> 01:23:26,803
and began researching poverty...

1933
01:23:27,239 --> 01:23:28,539
that it finally clicked that I was poor.

1934
01:23:29,975 --> 01:23:32,209
"I grew up in poverty."

1935
01:23:32,679 --> 01:23:36,080
Being around people
with financial safety nets,

1936
01:23:36,081 --> 01:23:39,817
I'm forced to confront
that reality face-to-face.

1937
01:23:39,818 --> 01:23:41,385
It was such a disorienting realization.

1938
01:23:41,386 --> 01:23:44,488
I had honestly convinced myself
I was doing just fine.

1939
01:23:44,822 --> 01:23:46,691
The truth is,
I've received so much grace...

1940
01:23:46,692 --> 01:23:48,392
from people all throughout my life.

1941
01:23:48,393 --> 01:23:49,893
People have helped me along the way,

1942
01:23:49,894 --> 01:23:52,196
and I try to help others
without expecting a thing in return.

1943
01:23:52,798 --> 01:23:55,199
My mom always drilled that into me.

1944
01:23:55,634 --> 01:23:57,968
She always seemed to have someone
looking out for her.

1945
01:23:58,336 --> 01:24:01,505
Whenever she hit rock bottom,
someone would step in and lend a hand.

1946
01:24:01,907 --> 01:24:03,341
I look at my own life the same way.

1947
01:24:03,342 --> 01:24:05,109
At every critical crossroads,

1948
01:24:05,377 --> 01:24:06,477
people stepped up for me
out of pure kindness.

1949
01:24:07,546 --> 01:24:09,561
That's probably...

1950
01:24:12,017 --> 01:24:14,032
the only reason
I got those opportunities...

1951
01:24:14,453 --> 01:24:15,853
and managed to make it this far.

1952
01:24:23,929 --> 01:24:27,497
The players have gotten a massive payout
from today's challenge.

1953
01:24:29,201 --> 01:24:31,903
How will the dynamics shift...

1954
01:24:32,337 --> 01:24:34,972
now that real money and power have
entered the house?

1955
01:24:37,876 --> 01:24:40,544
Nangja, let's circle back...

1956
01:24:40,545 --> 01:24:43,414
to that theory I floated earlier.

1957
01:24:44,216 --> 01:24:45,616
A spy is in play,

1958
01:24:46,585 --> 01:24:49,687
and the Reporter holds all the cards.

1959
01:24:50,455 --> 01:24:51,889
- Want to take this somewhere private?
- Yeah.

1960
01:24:54,192 --> 01:24:55,893
Nobody can see us if we sit further back.

1961
01:25:00,232 --> 01:25:03,601
- That must've put you in a tough spot.
- Why do you say that?

1962
01:25:04,569 --> 01:25:08,472
The Reporter holds every piece
of leverage on the spy.

1963
01:25:08,907 --> 01:25:10,274
Play your cards right,

1964
01:25:11,109 --> 01:25:13,811
and you become the kingmaker.

1965
01:25:14,246 --> 01:25:16,280
Let me tell you something
you need to know.

1966
01:25:25,557 --> 01:25:28,448
(Nangja: Right, Equalism,
Working-class, Conservative)
